window.BAHR_DATA=window.BAHR_DATA||{};window.BAHR_DATA["passages-113"]=[{"id":"K113.01","q":"113:1–5","s":113,"surah":"al-Falaq","surah_ar":"الفلق","title":"al-Falaq · Sūrat al-Falaq","cairo":"K IV:820–822 · T XVI:644–651","source_page_first":820,"source_page_last":822,"chunk":"passages-113","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"Text and explanation","html":"<p>Sūrat al-Falaq. There is disagreement about it [whether Meccan or Medinan]; it has five verses.\nIn the name of God, the All-Merciful, the Merciful.\n“Say: I seek refuge in the Lord of daybreak, from the evil of what He created; from the evil of a darkening one when it enters; from the evil of those who blow upon knots; and from the evil of an envier when he envies.” [113:1–5]\n[TEXT VARIANT: The separate four-volume transcription&#39;s heading says “Meccan; also said to be Medinan,” adds “revealed after al-Fīl,” and repeats the basmala (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">بسملة</bdi>). Dubai&#39;s base heading is translated above.]</p>","author":"kashshaf","source_id":"K113.01"}],"types":["exposition","language","theology","reports"],"ar":"مختلف فيها، وهي خمس آيات\n\nبسم الله الرحمن الرحيم\n\n﴿قُلْ أَعُوذُ بِرَبِّ الْفَلَقِ (1) مِنْ شَرِّ ما خَلَقَ (2) وَمِنْ شَرِّ غاسِقٍ إِذا وَقَبَ (3) وَمِنْ شَرِّ النَّفَّاثاتِ فِي الْعُقَدِ (4) وَمِنْ شَرِّ حاسِدٍ إِذا حَسَدَ﴾ [1 - 5]","layers":[{"id":"K113.01","heading":"[K113.01 | T XVI 644; K IV 820]","text":"Sūrat al-Falaq. There is disagreement about it [whether Meccan or Medinan]; it has five verses.\nIn the name of God, the All-Merciful, the Merciful.\n“Say: I seek refuge in the Lord of daybreak, from the evil of what He created; from the evil of a darkening one when it enters; from the evil of those who blow upon knots; and from the evil of an envier when he envies.” [113:1–5]\n[TEXT VARIANT: The separate four-volume transcription's heading says “Meccan; also said to be Medinan,” adds “revealed after al-Fīl,” and repeats the basmala (بسملة). Dubai's base heading is translated above.]","title":"Text and explanation","author":"kashshaf","surah":113,"ar":"مختلف فيها، وهي خمس آيات\n\nبسم الله الرحمن الرحيم\n\n﴿قُلْ أَعُوذُ بِرَبِّ الْفَلَقِ (1) مِنْ شَرِّ ما خَلَقَ (2) وَمِنْ شَرِّ غاسِقٍ إِذا وَقَبَ (3) وَمِنْ شَرِّ النَّفَّاثاتِ فِي الْعُقَدِ (4) وَمِنْ شَرِّ حاسِدٍ إِذا حَسَدَ﴾ [1 - 5]","html":"<p>Sūrat al-Falaq. There is disagreement about it [whether Meccan or Medinan]; it has five verses.\nIn the name of God, the All-Merciful, the Merciful.\n“Say: I seek refuge in the Lord of daybreak, from the evil of what He created; from the evil of a darkening one when it enters; from the evil of those who blow upon knots; and from the evil of an envier when he envies.” [113:1–5]\n[TEXT VARIANT: The separate four-volume transcription&#39;s heading says “Meccan; also said to be Medinan,” adds “revealed after al-Fīl,” and repeats the basmala (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">بسملة</bdi>). Dubai&#39;s base heading is translated above.]</p>","lemma":"","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/final-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true,"display_normalization":"Reversed electronic reference brackets normalized; source text preserved in the dossier."}}],"cairo_notes":[],"verses":[{"key":"113:1","number":1,"ar":"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَـٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ قُلْ أَعُوذُ بِرَبِّ ٱلْفَلَقِ","en":"Say: I seek refuge in the Lord of the Daybreak"},{"key":"113:2","number":2,"ar":"مِن شَرِّ مَا خَلَقَ","en":"From the evil of that which He created"},{"key":"113:3","number":3,"ar":"وَمِن شَرِّ غَاسِقٍ إِذَا وَقَبَ","en":"From the evil of the darkness when it is intense"},{"key":"113:4","number":4,"ar":"وَمِن شَرِّ ٱلنَّفَّـٰثَـٰتِ فِى ٱلْعُقَدِ","en":"And from the evil of malignant witchcraft"},{"key":"113:5","number":5,"ar":"وَمِن شَرِّ حَاسِدٍ إِذَا حَسَدَ","en":"And from the evil of the envier when he envieth"}],"quran_ar":"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَـٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ قُلْ أَعُوذُ بِرَبِّ ٱلْفَلَقِ ﴿1﴾ مِن شَرِّ مَا خَلَقَ ﴿2﴾ وَمِن شَرِّ غَاسِقٍ إِذَا وَقَبَ ﴿3﴾ وَمِن شَرِّ ٱلنَّفَّـٰثَـٰتِ فِى ٱلْعُقَدِ ﴿4﴾ وَمِن شَرِّ حَاسِدٍ إِذَا حَسَدَ ﴿5﴾","quran_en":"1. Say: I seek refuge in the Lord of the Daybreak 2. From the evil of that which He created 3. From the evil of the darkness when it is intense 4. And from the evil of malignant witchcraft 5. And from the evil of the envier when he envieth"},{"id":"K113.02","q":"113:1","s":113,"surah":"al-Falaq","surah_ar":"الفلق","title":"al-Falaq · Al-falaq and al-faraq mean morning, because night is split and parted away from it: faʿal has the me","cairo":"K IV:820–822 · T XVI:644–651","source_page_first":820,"source_page_last":822,"chunk":"passages-113","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"Text and explanation","html":"<p>Al-falaq and al-faraq (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الفرق</bdi>) mean morning, because night is split and parted away from it: faʿal (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فَعَل</bdi>) has the meaning of mafʿūl (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">مفعول</bdi>). The proverb says, “It is clearer than the breaking of morning,” or “than the parting of morning.” Related to this is their expression saṭaʿa al-furqān (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">سطع الفرقان</bdi>) when dawn rises.\nIt is also said to mean everything God splits open: the earth for plants, mountains for springs, clouds for rain, wombs for children, grain and date-stones, and other things. It is also said to be a valley in Hell or a pit within it, from their use of falaq (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فلق</bdi>) for a depression in the ground; the plural is fulqān (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فلقان</bdi>).\nIt is reported that one of the Companions came to Syria and saw the houses of the protected non-Muslim communities (ahl al-dhimma), their comfortable lives and the worldly plenty granted them. He said, “I do not mind. Is al-falaq (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الفلق</bdi>) not awaiting them?” He was asked, “What is al-falaq (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الفلق</bdi>)?” He answered: “A chamber in Hell which, when opened, makes all the inhabitants of the Fire cry out at the intensity of its heat.”</p>","author":"kashshaf","source_id":"K113.02"},{"type":"exposition","title":"“Because night is split away from it”","html":"<p>Sūrat al-Falaq. Meccan; also said to be Medinan. Five verses.\nIn the name of God, the All-Merciful, the Merciful.\nHis words, “Because night is split away from it”: that is, night splits away from morning and morning emerges. Faʿal has the meaning of mafʿūl (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">مفعول</bdi>): night is what is split away from it.\nHis words, “It is also said to mean everything He splits open.” Al-Qāḍī says: “It includes all possible beings, for He split the darkness of nonexistence away from them with the light of bringing into existence—particularly what emerges from an origin, such as springs, rains, plants and children. In conventional use it is especially morning, which is why it was explained that way. Morning is singled out because it changes the condition, replaces the loneliness of night with the joy of light, [resembles the opening of the Day of Resurrection], and signals that the one able to remove the darkness of night from this world can remove from the person seeking refuge what he fears. The word Lord is more fitting here than the other names, because protection from harms is an act of nurture.”\n[SOURCE CHECK T113-U1: Ṭībī&#39;s quoted text has the difficult <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">محاكاة الخير بيوم القيامة</bdi> and the electronic text ends with <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">قريبة</bdi>. Al-Bayḍāwī, Anwār al-tanzīl V:348, reads <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">محاكاة فاتحة يوم القيامة</bdi> and <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">تربية</bdi>. The bracketed resurrection clause follows that quoted source; <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">تربية</bdi> also corroborates the earlier provisional reading. These are source-based restorations, not a newly collated Ṭībī manuscript reading.]</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T113.01"},{"type":"exposition","title":"“I do not mind. Is al-falaq not awaiting them?”","html":"<p>His words, “I do not mind. Is al-falaq (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الفلق</bdi>) not awaiting them?”: that is, I do not mind the beauty of their houses and the ease of their lives. He then begins a new interrogative sentence to elicit assent: “Is al-falaq (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الفلق</bdi>) not awaiting them?”\nComparable is what we transmitted from al-Bukhārī, Muslim, Aḥmad, al-Tirmidhī and al-Nasāʾī, from Ibn ʿAbbās in a long report from ʿUmar—may God be pleased with him: “I entered upon the Messenger of God ﷺ and greeted him. He was reclining upon the woven strands of a mat which had left marks upon his side. I sat down and lifted my head to look about the house. By God, I saw nothing there to catch the eye except three hides. I said: ‘Messenger of God, pray that God grant plenty to your community: He has granted plenty to Persia and Rome, although they do not worship God.’ He sat upright and said, ‘Are you in doubt, son of al-Khaṭṭāb? These are people whose good things have been given them in advance in this worldly life.’ I said: ‘Ask forgiveness for me, Messenger of God.’” The report [continues].\nAs for interpreting al-falaq (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الفلق</bdi>) as a valley in Hell, Muḥyī al-Sunna transmits one report from Ibn ʿAbbās that al-falaq (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الفلق</bdi>) is a prison in Hell, and from al-Kalbī that it is a valley in Hell.\n[TEXT CORRECTION T113-C4: OpenITI contains nonsensical extra words after “his side.” They are absent from the inspected scan p.645 and are not translated.]</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T113.02"}],"types":["exposition","language","theology","reports"],"ar":"الفلق والفرق: الصبح، لأن الليل يفلق عنه ويفرق: فعل بمعنى مفعول. يقال في المثل: هو أبين من فلق الصبح، ومن فرق الصبح. ومنه قولهم: سطع الفرقان، إذا طلع الفجر. وقيل: هو كل ما يفلقه الله،\n\nكالأرض عن النبات، والجبال عن العيون، والسحاب عن المطر، والأرحام عن الأولاد، والحب والنوى وغير ذلك. وقيل: هو واٍد في جهنم أو جب فيها، من قولهم لما اطمأن من الأرض: الفلق، والجمع: فلقان. وعن بعض الصحابة أنه قدم الشام فرأى دور أهل الذمّة وما هم فيه من خفض العيش، وما وسع عليهم من دنياهم، فقال: لا أبالي، أليس من ورائهم الفلق؟ فقيل: وما الفلق؟\n\nقال: بيت في جهنم إذا فتح صاح جميع أهل النار من شدّة حرّه.\n\nمكية، وقيل مدنية وهي خمس آيات\n\nبسم الله الرحمن الرحيم\n\nقولُه: (لأن الليلَ يُفلَقُ عنه)، أي: لأن الليلَ يَنْشقُّ عن الصبحِ، فيخرجُ الصبح؛ فَعَلٌ بمعنى مفعول؛ فالليلُ مفلوقٌ عنه.\n\nقولُه: (وقيل: هو كلُّ ما يَفْلِقُه)، قال القاضي: «وهو يَعمُّ جميعَ الممكنات؛ فإنه تعالى فَلَقَ ظلمةَ العدمِ بنورِ الإيجادِ عنها، سيّما ما يخرجُ عن أصلٍ، كالعيونِ والأمطارِ والنباتِ والأولاد، ويَختْصُّ عُرفًا بالصُّبح، ولذلك فُسِّر به. وتَخْصيصُه لِما فيه من تَغيُرِ الحالِ، وتَبدُّلِ وحشةِ\n\nالليلِ بسرورِ النور، [ومحاكاة فاتحة يوم القيامة]، والإشعارِ بأن مَن قدَر أن يزيلَ ظلمةَ الليلِ عن هذا العالم، قدَر أن يزيلَ عن العائذِ ما يخافه. ولفظُ الرّبّ ها هنا أوقعُ من سائرِ الأسماء، لأن الإعاذةَ مِن المضارِّ تربية».\n\nقولُه: (لا أبالي، أليسَ من ورائهم الفَلَق؟ )، أي: لا أبالي بحُسنِ دُورِهم وخفضِ عَيْشِهم.\n\nثم استأنفَ مستفهمًا على سبيل التقرير: أليسَ من ورائهم الفَلَق؟ ونظيرُه ما روينا البخاريِّ ومسلمٍ وأحمدَ والترمذيِّ والنسائي، عن ابنِ عباسٍ في حديثٍ طويل، عن عمرَ رضي الله عنه: دخلتُ على رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم، فسلّمتُ وهو متكئ على رمالٍ حصيرٍ قد أثر في جنبِه فجلستُ فرفعتُ رأسي في البيت، فوالله ما رأيتُ فيه شيئًا ردَّ البصرَ إلا أَهَبَةَّ ثلاثةً، فقال: يا رسولَ الله، ادع الله أن يوسعَ على أمتك، فقد وَسَّعَ على فارسَ والرومِ وهم لا يعبدون الله، فاستوى جالسًا، ثم قالَ: أفي شكٍّ أنت يا ابنَ الخطاب؟ أولئك قومٌ قد عُجِّلتْ لهم طيباتُهم في الحياة الدنيا.\n\nفقلت: استغفِرْ لي يا رسولَ الله. الحديث. وأما تفسير الفلقِ بأنه وادٍ في جهنم، فروى محيي السُّنةِ عن ابن عباسٍ في رواية، أن الفلقَ سَجْنٌ في جهنم، وعن الكلبي أنه وادٍ في جهنم.","layers":[{"id":"K113.02","heading":"[K113.02 | T XVI 644–646; K IV 820]","text":"Al-falaq and al-faraq (الفرق) mean morning, because night is split and parted away from it: faʿal (فَعَل) has the meaning of mafʿūl (مفعول). The proverb says, “It is clearer than the breaking of morning,” or “than the parting of morning.” Related to this is their expression saṭaʿa al-furqān (سطع الفرقان) when dawn rises.\nIt is also said to mean everything God splits open: the earth for plants, mountains for springs, clouds for rain, wombs for children, grain and date-stones, and other things. It is also said to be a valley in Hell or a pit within it, from their use of falaq (فلق) for a depression in the ground; the plural is fulqān (فلقان).\nIt is reported that one of the Companions came to Syria and saw the houses of the protected non-Muslim communities (ahl al-dhimma), their comfortable lives and the worldly plenty granted them. He said, “I do not mind. Is al-falaq (الفلق) not awaiting them?” He was asked, “What is al-falaq (الفلق)?” He answered: “A chamber in Hell which, when opened, makes all the inhabitants of the Fire cry out at the intensity of its heat.”","title":"Text and explanation","author":"kashshaf","surah":113,"ar":"الفلق والفرق: الصبح، لأن الليل يفلق عنه ويفرق: فعل بمعنى مفعول. يقال في المثل: هو أبين من فلق الصبح، ومن فرق الصبح. ومنه قولهم: سطع الفرقان، إذا طلع الفجر. وقيل: هو كل ما يفلقه الله،\n\nكالأرض عن النبات، والجبال عن العيون، والسحاب عن المطر، والأرحام عن الأولاد، والحب والنوى وغير ذلك. وقيل: هو واٍد في جهنم أو جب فيها، من قولهم لما اطمأن من الأرض: الفلق، والجمع: فلقان. وعن بعض الصحابة أنه قدم الشام فرأى دور أهل الذمّة وما هم فيه من خفض العيش، وما وسع عليهم من دنياهم، فقال: لا أبالي، أليس من ورائهم الفلق؟ فقيل: وما الفلق؟\n\nقال: بيت في جهنم إذا فتح صاح جميع أهل النار من شدّة حرّه.","html":"<p>Al-falaq and al-faraq (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الفرق</bdi>) mean morning, because night is split and parted away from it: faʿal (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فَعَل</bdi>) has the meaning of mafʿūl (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">مفعول</bdi>). The proverb says, “It is clearer than the breaking of morning,” or “than the parting of morning.” Related to this is their expression saṭaʿa al-furqān (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">سطع الفرقان</bdi>) when dawn rises.\nIt is also said to mean everything God splits open: the earth for plants, mountains for springs, clouds for rain, wombs for children, grain and date-stones, and other things. It is also said to be a valley in Hell or a pit within it, from their use of falaq (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فلق</bdi>) for a depression in the ground; the plural is fulqān (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فلقان</bdi>).\nIt is reported that one of the Companions came to Syria and saw the houses of the protected non-Muslim communities (ahl al-dhimma), their comfortable lives and the worldly plenty granted them. He said, “I do not mind. Is al-falaq (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الفلق</bdi>) not awaiting them?” He was asked, “What is al-falaq (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الفلق</bdi>)?” He answered: “A chamber in Hell which, when opened, makes all the inhabitants of the Fire cry out at the intensity of its heat.”</p>","lemma":"","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/final-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}},{"id":"T113.01","heading":"[T113.01 | XVI 644–645 | on K113.02]","text":"Sūrat al-Falaq. Meccan; also said to be Medinan. Five verses.\nIn the name of God, the All-Merciful, the Merciful.\nHis words, “Because night is split away from it”: that is, night splits away from morning and morning emerges. Faʿal has the meaning of mafʿūl (مفعول): night is what is split away from it.\nHis words, “It is also said to mean everything He splits open.” Al-Qāḍī says: “It includes all possible beings, for He split the darkness of nonexistence away from them with the light of bringing into existence—particularly what emerges from an origin, such as springs, rains, plants and children. In conventional use it is especially morning, which is why it was explained that way. Morning is singled out because it changes the condition, replaces the loneliness of night with the joy of light, [resembles the opening of the Day of Resurrection], and signals that the one able to remove the darkness of night from this world can remove from the person seeking refuge what he fears. The word Lord is more fitting here than the other names, because protection from harms is an act of nurture.”\n[SOURCE CHECK T113-U1: Ṭībī's quoted text has the difficult محاكاة الخير بيوم القيامة and the electronic text ends with قريبة. Al-Bayḍāwī, Anwār al-tanzīl V:348, reads محاكاة فاتحة يوم القيامة and تربية. The bracketed resurrection clause follows that quoted source; تربية also corroborates the earlier provisional reading. These are source-based restorations, not a newly collated Ṭībī manuscript reading.]","title":"“Because night is split away from it”","author":"tibi","on":["K113.02"],"anchor":"K113.02","surah":113,"ar":"مكية، وقيل مدنية وهي خمس آيات\n\nبسم الله الرحمن الرحيم\n\nقولُه: (لأن الليلَ يُفلَقُ عنه)، أي: لأن الليلَ يَنْشقُّ عن الصبحِ، فيخرجُ الصبح؛ فَعَلٌ بمعنى مفعول؛ فالليلُ مفلوقٌ عنه.\n\nقولُه: (وقيل: هو كلُّ ما يَفْلِقُه)، قال القاضي: «وهو يَعمُّ جميعَ الممكنات؛ فإنه تعالى فَلَقَ ظلمةَ العدمِ بنورِ الإيجادِ عنها، سيّما ما يخرجُ عن أصلٍ، كالعيونِ والأمطارِ والنباتِ والأولاد، ويَختْصُّ عُرفًا بالصُّبح، ولذلك فُسِّر به. وتَخْصيصُه لِما فيه من تَغيُرِ الحالِ، وتَبدُّلِ وحشةِ\n\nالليلِ بسرورِ النور، [ومحاكاة فاتحة يوم القيامة]، والإشعارِ بأن مَن قدَر أن يزيلَ ظلمةَ الليلِ عن هذا العالم، قدَر أن يزيلَ عن العائذِ ما يخافه. ولفظُ الرّبّ ها هنا أوقعُ من سائرِ الأسماء، لأن الإعاذةَ مِن المضارِّ تربية».","html":"<p>Sūrat al-Falaq. Meccan; also said to be Medinan. Five verses.\nIn the name of God, the All-Merciful, the Merciful.\nHis words, “Because night is split away from it”: that is, night splits away from morning and morning emerges. Faʿal has the meaning of mafʿūl (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">مفعول</bdi>): night is what is split away from it.\nHis words, “It is also said to mean everything He splits open.” Al-Qāḍī says: “It includes all possible beings, for He split the darkness of nonexistence away from them with the light of bringing into existence—particularly what emerges from an origin, such as springs, rains, plants and children. In conventional use it is especially morning, which is why it was explained that way. Morning is singled out because it changes the condition, replaces the loneliness of night with the joy of light, [resembles the opening of the Day of Resurrection], and signals that the one able to remove the darkness of night from this world can remove from the person seeking refuge what he fears. The word Lord is more fitting here than the other names, because protection from harms is an act of nurture.”\n[SOURCE CHECK T113-U1: Ṭībī&#39;s quoted text has the difficult <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">محاكاة الخير بيوم القيامة</bdi> and the electronic text ends with <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">قريبة</bdi>. Al-Bayḍāwī, Anwār al-tanzīl V:348, reads <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">محاكاة فاتحة يوم القيامة</bdi> and <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">تربية</bdi>. The bracketed resurrection clause follows that quoted source; <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">تربية</bdi> also corroborates the earlier provisional reading. These are source-based restorations, not a newly collated Ṭībī manuscript reading.]</p>","lemma":"لأن الليل يفلق عنه","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/final-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}},{"id":"T113.02","heading":"[T113.02 | XVI 645 | on K113.02]","text":"His words, “I do not mind. Is al-falaq (الفلق) not awaiting them?”: that is, I do not mind the beauty of their houses and the ease of their lives. He then begins a new interrogative sentence to elicit assent: “Is al-falaq (الفلق) not awaiting them?”\nComparable is what we transmitted from al-Bukhārī, Muslim, Aḥmad, al-Tirmidhī and al-Nasāʾī, from Ibn ʿAbbās in a long report from ʿUmar—may God be pleased with him: “I entered upon the Messenger of God ﷺ and greeted him. He was reclining upon the woven strands of a mat which had left marks upon his side. I sat down and lifted my head to look about the house. By God, I saw nothing there to catch the eye except three hides. I said: ‘Messenger of God, pray that God grant plenty to your community: He has granted plenty to Persia and Rome, although they do not worship God.’ He sat upright and said, ‘Are you in doubt, son of al-Khaṭṭāb? These are people whose good things have been given them in advance in this worldly life.’ I said: ‘Ask forgiveness for me, Messenger of God.’” The report [continues].\nAs for interpreting al-falaq (الفلق) as a valley in Hell, Muḥyī al-Sunna transmits one report from Ibn ʿAbbās that al-falaq (الفلق) is a prison in Hell, and from al-Kalbī that it is a valley in Hell.\n[TEXT CORRECTION T113-C4: OpenITI contains nonsensical extra words after “his side.” They are absent from the inspected scan p.645 and are not translated.]","title":"“I do not mind. Is al-falaq not awaiting them?”","author":"tibi","on":["K113.02"],"anchor":"K113.02","surah":113,"ar":"قولُه: (لا أبالي، أليسَ من ورائهم الفَلَق؟ )، أي: لا أبالي بحُسنِ دُورِهم وخفضِ عَيْشِهم.\n\nثم استأنفَ مستفهمًا على سبيل التقرير: أليسَ من ورائهم الفَلَق؟ ونظيرُه ما روينا البخاريِّ ومسلمٍ وأحمدَ والترمذيِّ والنسائي، عن ابنِ عباسٍ في حديثٍ طويل، عن عمرَ رضي الله عنه: دخلتُ على رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم، فسلّمتُ وهو متكئ على رمالٍ حصيرٍ قد أثر في جنبِه فجلستُ فرفعتُ رأسي في البيت، فوالله ما رأيتُ فيه شيئًا ردَّ البصرَ إلا أَهَبَةَّ ثلاثةً، فقال: يا رسولَ الله، ادع الله أن يوسعَ على أمتك، فقد وَسَّعَ على فارسَ والرومِ وهم لا يعبدون الله، فاستوى جالسًا، ثم قالَ: أفي شكٍّ أنت يا ابنَ الخطاب؟ أولئك قومٌ قد عُجِّلتْ لهم طيباتُهم في الحياة الدنيا.\n\nفقلت: استغفِرْ لي يا رسولَ الله. الحديث. وأما تفسير الفلقِ بأنه وادٍ في جهنم، فروى محيي السُّنةِ عن ابن عباسٍ في رواية، أن الفلقَ سَجْنٌ في جهنم، وعن الكلبي أنه وادٍ في جهنم.","html":"<p>His words, “I do not mind. Is al-falaq (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الفلق</bdi>) not awaiting them?”: that is, I do not mind the beauty of their houses and the ease of their lives. He then begins a new interrogative sentence to elicit assent: “Is al-falaq (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الفلق</bdi>) not awaiting them?”\nComparable is what we transmitted from al-Bukhārī, Muslim, Aḥmad, al-Tirmidhī and al-Nasāʾī, from Ibn ʿAbbās in a long report from ʿUmar—may God be pleased with him: “I entered upon the Messenger of God ﷺ and greeted him. He was reclining upon the woven strands of a mat which had left marks upon his side. I sat down and lifted my head to look about the house. By God, I saw nothing there to catch the eye except three hides. I said: ‘Messenger of God, pray that God grant plenty to your community: He has granted plenty to Persia and Rome, although they do not worship God.’ He sat upright and said, ‘Are you in doubt, son of al-Khaṭṭāb? These are people whose good things have been given them in advance in this worldly life.’ I said: ‘Ask forgiveness for me, Messenger of God.’” The report [continues].\nAs for interpreting al-falaq (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الفلق</bdi>) as a valley in Hell, Muḥyī al-Sunna transmits one report from Ibn ʿAbbās that al-falaq (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الفلق</bdi>) is a prison in Hell, and from al-Kalbī that it is a valley in Hell.\n[TEXT CORRECTION T113-C4: OpenITI contains nonsensical extra words after “his side.” They are absent from the inspected scan p.645 and are not translated.]</p>","lemma":"لا أبالي، أليس من ورائهم الفلق؟ ","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/final-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}}],"cairo_notes":[],"verses":[{"key":"113:1","number":1,"ar":"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَـٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ قُلْ أَعُوذُ بِرَبِّ ٱلْفَلَقِ","en":"Say: I seek refuge in the Lord of the Daybreak"}],"quran_ar":"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَـٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ قُلْ أَعُوذُ بِرَبِّ ٱلْفَلَقِ ﴿1﴾","quran_en":"1. Say: I seek refuge in the Lord of the Daybreak"},{"id":"K113.03","q":"113:2","s":113,"surah":"al-Falaq","surah_ar":"الفلق","title":"al-Falaq · “From the evil of what He created”: from the evil of His creation","cairo":"K IV:820–822 · T XVI:644–651","source_page_first":820,"source_page_last":822,"chunk":"passages-113","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"Text and explanation","html":"<p>“From the evil of what He created”: from the evil of His creation. Their evil consists in the disobedience and sins committed by morally responsible living beings, and the harm they do one another through injustice, aggression, killing, striking, abuse and so forth; in what the non-responsible among living beings do by devouring, tearing with the teeth, stinging and biting, such as predatory animals and creeping creatures; and in the kinds of harm God has placed in inanimate things, such as burning in fire and killing in poison.</p>","author":"kashshaf","source_id":"K113.03"},{"type":"exposition","title":"“Their evil is what morally responsible living beings do”","html":"<p>His words, “Their evil is what morally responsible living beings do.” Perhaps “among living beings” is supplied as an explanation of “the morally responsible” in order to exclude the angels.\nAl-Qāḍī says: “The world of creation is singled out as that from which refuge is sought because evil is confined to it: the world of command is wholly good. Its evil is either voluntary—confined to its agent or extending to others, as in unbelief and injustice—or natural, as in fire&#39;s burning and poisons&#39; destruction.”</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T113.03"}],"types":["exposition","language","theology","reports"],"ar":"(مِنْ شَرِّ ما خَلَقَ) من شر خلقه، وشرهم: ما يفعله المكلفون من الحيوان من المعاصي والمآثم، ومضارّة بعضهم بعضًا من ظلم وبغى وقتل وضرب وشتم وغير ذلك، وما يفعله غير المكلفين منه عن الأكل والنهس واللدغ والعض كالسباع والحشرات، وما وضعه الله في الموات من أنواع الضرر كالإحراق في النار والقتل في السم.\n\nقولُه: (وشَرُّهم ما يفعله المكلَّفون من الحيوان)، لعلَّ إيقاعَ \"من الحيوان\" بيانًا للمكلَّفين، لإخراجِ الملائكةِ منهم. قال القاضي: «خُصَّ عالمُ الخلقِ بالاستعاذةِ عنه لانحصارِ الشرِّ فيه؛ فإن عالمَ الأمرِ خيرٌ كلُّه، وشَرُّه اختياريٌ لازمٌ ومتعدٍّ، كالكفرِ والظلم، وطبيعيٌ كإحراقِ النارِ وإهلاكِ السموم».","layers":[{"id":"K113.03","heading":"[K113.03 | T XVI 646; K IV 820]","text":"“From the evil of what He created”: from the evil of His creation. Their evil consists in the disobedience and sins committed by morally responsible living beings, and the harm they do one another through injustice, aggression, killing, striking, abuse and so forth; in what the non-responsible among living beings do by devouring, tearing with the teeth, stinging and biting, such as predatory animals and creeping creatures; and in the kinds of harm God has placed in inanimate things, such as burning in fire and killing in poison.","title":"Text and explanation","author":"kashshaf","surah":113,"ar":"(مِنْ شَرِّ ما خَلَقَ) من شر خلقه، وشرهم: ما يفعله المكلفون من الحيوان من المعاصي والمآثم، ومضارّة بعضهم بعضًا من ظلم وبغى وقتل وضرب وشتم وغير ذلك، وما يفعله غير المكلفين منه عن الأكل والنهس واللدغ والعض كالسباع والحشرات، وما وضعه الله في الموات من أنواع الضرر كالإحراق في النار والقتل في السم.","html":"<p>“From the evil of what He created”: from the evil of His creation. Their evil consists in the disobedience and sins committed by morally responsible living beings, and the harm they do one another through injustice, aggression, killing, striking, abuse and so forth; in what the non-responsible among living beings do by devouring, tearing with the teeth, stinging and biting, such as predatory animals and creeping creatures; and in the kinds of harm God has placed in inanimate things, such as burning in fire and killing in poison.</p>","lemma":"","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/final-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}},{"id":"T113.03","heading":"[T113.03 | XVI 646 | on K113.03]","text":"His words, “Their evil is what morally responsible living beings do.” Perhaps “among living beings” is supplied as an explanation of “the morally responsible” in order to exclude the angels.\nAl-Qāḍī says: “The world of creation is singled out as that from which refuge is sought because evil is confined to it: the world of command is wholly good. Its evil is either voluntary—confined to its agent or extending to others, as in unbelief and injustice—or natural, as in fire's burning and poisons' destruction.”","title":"“Their evil is what morally responsible living beings do”","author":"tibi","on":["K113.03"],"anchor":"K113.03","surah":113,"ar":"قولُه: (وشَرُّهم ما يفعله المكلَّفون من الحيوان)، لعلَّ إيقاعَ \"من الحيوان\" بيانًا للمكلَّفين، لإخراجِ الملائكةِ منهم. قال القاضي: «خُصَّ عالمُ الخلقِ بالاستعاذةِ عنه لانحصارِ الشرِّ فيه؛ فإن عالمَ الأمرِ خيرٌ كلُّه، وشَرُّه اختياريٌ لازمٌ ومتعدٍّ، كالكفرِ والظلم، وطبيعيٌ كإحراقِ النارِ وإهلاكِ السموم».","html":"<p>His words, “Their evil is what morally responsible living beings do.” Perhaps “among living beings” is supplied as an explanation of “the morally responsible” in order to exclude the angels.\nAl-Qāḍī says: “The world of creation is singled out as that from which refuge is sought because evil is confined to it: the world of command is wholly good. Its evil is either voluntary—confined to its agent or extending to others, as in unbelief and injustice—or natural, as in fire&#39;s burning and poisons&#39; destruction.”</p>","lemma":"وشرهم ما يفعله المكلفون من الحيوان","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/final-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}}],"cairo_notes":[],"verses":[{"key":"113:2","number":2,"ar":"مِن شَرِّ مَا خَلَقَ","en":"From the evil of that which He created"}],"quran_ar":"مِن شَرِّ مَا خَلَقَ ﴿2﴾","quran_en":"2. From the evil of that which He created"},{"id":"K113.04","q":"113:3","s":113,"surah":"al-Falaq","surah_ar":"الفلق","title":"al-Falaq · Al-ghāsiq is night when its darkness becomes dense, from His statement “… until the darkness of nigh","cairo":"K IV:820–822 · T XVI:644–651","source_page_first":820,"source_page_last":822,"chunk":"passages-113","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"Text and explanation","html":"<p>Al-ghāsiq is night when its darkness becomes dense, from His statement “… until the darkness of night” [17:78]. Related expressions are “the eye ghasaqat”—filled with tears—and “the wound ghasaqat”—filled with blood. Its wuqūb (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">وقوب</bdi>) is the entry of its darkness into everything. One says waqabat al-shams when the sun sets. In the report, when he saw that the sun had set, he said, “This is the time when it becomes lawful”—meaning the sunset prayer.\nIt is also said to be the moon when full. ʿĀʾisha—may God be pleased with her—reports: the Messenger of God ﷺ took my hand, pointed to the moon, and said, “Seek refuge in God from the evil of this, for it is the darkening one when it enters.” Its wuqūb (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">وقوب</bdi>) is its entering eclipse and becoming dark.\nAl-ghāsiq may also mean a black snake; its waqb is its striking and piercing. Waqb means a hole, as in waqbat al-tharīd (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الثريد</bdi>), the hollow in a dish of bread soaked in broth.\nRefuge is sought from the evil of night because evil spreads more widely in it and is harder to guard against. Hence their sayings, “Night conceals misfortune better,” and “Night has become treacherous.” When it grows dark, treachery increases. Evil is attributed to night because it accompanies it, occurring within it.\n[TEXT VARIANT K113-C1: Dubai p.646 cites the opening of 17:78, “Establish prayer at the sun&#39;s decline,” rather than the words containing ghasaq. The independent four-volume transcription and Turkish p.1470 give the relevant latter clause, translated above. Both forms are recorded.]</p>","author":"kashshaf","source_id":"K113.04"},{"type":"exposition","title":"“When its darkness becomes dense”","html":"<p>His words, “When its darkness becomes dense.” Al-Jawharī: “Iʿtakara al-ẓalām: it became intermingled, as though part of it returned upon another because of the slowness of its clearing.”\nHis words, “One says waqabat al-shams when it sets.” Al-Rāghib: “Waqb is like a hollow in something; hence waqabat al-shams. Īqāb is its disappearing.”\nHis words, “This is the time when it becomes lawful”: ḥīn (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">حين</bdi>) is nominative; in ḥillihā (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">حِلّها</bdi>) the ḥāʾ (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ح</bdi>) has kasra (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">كَسْرة</bdi>) and the lām (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ل</bdi>) is genitive. Al-Nihāya: “In the report: when he saw that the sun had waqabat, he said, ‘This is the time of its lawfulness.’ Waqabat means ‘set’; the time of its lawfulness is the time when its performance becomes lawful, meaning the sunset prayer. Wuqūb means entry into anything.”</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T113.04"},{"type":"exposition","title":"“From ʿĀʾisha—may God be pleased with her”","html":"<p>His words, “From ʿĀʾisha—may God be pleased with her.” The report was transmitted by Imam Aḥmad and al-Tirmidhī; it does not contain “he took my hand.”\nThe Imam transmits from Ibn Qutayba: “The moon was called ghāsiq (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">غاسق</bdi>) because it is eclipsed and darkens: its light departs and it becomes black. Its wuqūb (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">وقوب</bdi>) is entering that blackness.” He also says: “It has been established that the moon is not luminous in its own body; it is therefore called ghāsiq (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">غاسق</bdi>). Its wuqūb (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">وقوب</bdi>) is the dark moon at the end of the month, when its strength is slight and it is at its lowest condition. For that reason sorcerers then occupy themselves with sorcery that produces illness. This accords with the occasion of revelation of the two sūrahs.” God knows best.\nHis words, “Night conceals misfortune better.” Al-Maydānī says: “That is, do what you wish at night, for it conceals your secret better. The first to say this was Sāriya ibn ʿUwaymir ibn ʿAdī al-ʿUqaylī.” Its occasion is mentioned in his book.\nHis words, “Night has become treacherous”: it is said to be like aḥṣada al-zarʿ (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أحصد الزرع</bdi>) [the crop became ready for harvest], that is, the time of its treachery has arrived; it is also said to mean “it has become possessed of treachery.”</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T113.05"}],"types":["exposition","language","theology","reports"],"ar":"و\"الغاسق\": الليل إذا اعتكر ظلامه، من قوله تعالى: (إِلَى غَسَقِ اللَّيْلِ) [ الإسراء: 78 ]ومنه: غسقت العين امتلأت دمعا، وغسقت الجراحة: امتلأت دمًا.\n\nووقوبه: دخول ظلامه في كل شيء، ويقال: وقبت الشمس إذا غابت. وفي الحديث: لما رأى الشمس قد وقبت قال: هذا حين حلها، يعنى صلاة المغرب. وقيل: هو القمر إذا امتلأ،\n\nوعن عائشة رضي الله عنها: أخذ رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم بيدي فأشار إلى القمر فقال: تعوّذي بالله من شر هذا، فإنه الغاسق إذا وقب، ووقوبه: دخوله في الكسوف واسوداده. ويجوز أن يراد بالغاسق: الأسود من الحيات، ووقبه: ضربه ونقبه. والوقب: النقب، ومنه: وقبة الثريد؛ والتعوّذ من شر الليل؛ لأن انبثاثه فيه أكثر، والتحرّز منه أصعب، ومنه قولهم: الليل أخفى للويل، وقولهم: أغدر الليل؛\n\nلأنه إذا أظلم كثر فيه الغدر، وأسند الشر إليه لملابسته له من حدوثه فيه.\n\nقولُه: (إذا اعتكر ظَلامُه)، الجوهري: «اعتكرَ الظلامُ: اختلطَ كأنه كرَّ بعضُه على بعض من بُطْءِ انجلائِه».\n\nقولُه: (ويقال: وَقَبت الشمس، إذا غابت)، الراغب: «الوَقَبُ كالنُقْرةِ في الشئ، ومنه وَقَبتِ الشمس، والإيقابُ: تَغيّبُها»\n\nقولُه: (هذا حينُ حَلِّها)، برفعِ \"حين\"، وكسرِ الحاءِ، وجرِّ اللامِ من \"حلها\". النهاية:\n\n«وفي الحديث: لّما رأى الشمسَ قد وَقَبتْ، قال: هذا حينُ حلِّها؛ وَقَبتْ: غابت. وحينُ حِلِّها: الوقتُ الذي يَحلُّ فيه أداؤها، يعني: صلاة المغرب. والوُقوبُ: الدخولُ في كلِّ شئ».\n\nقولُه: (وعن عائشة-رضي الله عنها)، الحديثُ اخرجَه الإمامُ أحمدُ والترمذيّ، وليسَ فيه: آخذٌ بيدي؛ روى الإمامُ عن ابنِ قتيبة: «إنما سُمي القمرُ غاسقًا، لأنه يُكْسفُ فيغسِق، أي يذهبُ ضوؤُه، ويَسْود، ووقوبُه: دخولُه في ذلك الاسوداد». وقال: «وقد صَحَّ أن القمرَ في جِرْمه غيرُ مستنير فسمّى بالغاسِق لهذا. ووقوبُه المحاقُ في آخر الشهر، لأنه حينئذٍ قليلُ القوةِ وفي غايةِ الرذالة، ولذلك يشتغلُ السحرةُ فيه بالسحر الذي يورثُ التمريض، وهذا مناسبٌ لسبب نزولِ السورتين «، والله أعلم.\n\nقولُه: (الليلُ أخفى للوَيْل)، قالَ الميداني: «أي: افعلْ ما تريدُ ليلًا، فإنه أسْترُ لسِرِّك. وأولُ مَن قال ذلك ساريةُ بنُ عُويْمرِ بنِ عَدِيٍّ العُقَيلي»، وسببُه مذكورٌ في كتابه.\n\nقولُه: (أَغدرَ الليل)، قيل: هو من بابِ أَحصدَ الزّرع، أي حانَ وقتُ غَدْره. وقيل: صارَ ذا غَدْر.","layers":[{"id":"K113.04","heading":"[K113.04 | T XVI 646–648; K IV 820–821]","text":"Al-ghāsiq is night when its darkness becomes dense, from His statement “… until the darkness of night” [17:78]. Related expressions are “the eye ghasaqat”—filled with tears—and “the wound ghasaqat”—filled with blood. Its wuqūb (وقوب) is the entry of its darkness into everything. One says waqabat al-shams when the sun sets. In the report, when he saw that the sun had set, he said, “This is the time when it becomes lawful”—meaning the sunset prayer.\nIt is also said to be the moon when full. ʿĀʾisha—may God be pleased with her—reports: the Messenger of God ﷺ took my hand, pointed to the moon, and said, “Seek refuge in God from the evil of this, for it is the darkening one when it enters.” Its wuqūb (وقوب) is its entering eclipse and becoming dark.\nAl-ghāsiq may also mean a black snake; its waqb is its striking and piercing. Waqb means a hole, as in waqbat al-tharīd (الثريد), the hollow in a dish of bread soaked in broth.\nRefuge is sought from the evil of night because evil spreads more widely in it and is harder to guard against. Hence their sayings, “Night conceals misfortune better,” and “Night has become treacherous.” When it grows dark, treachery increases. Evil is attributed to night because it accompanies it, occurring within it.\n[TEXT VARIANT K113-C1: Dubai p.646 cites the opening of 17:78, “Establish prayer at the sun's decline,” rather than the words containing ghasaq. The independent four-volume transcription and Turkish p.1470 give the relevant latter clause, translated above. Both forms are recorded.]","title":"Text and explanation","author":"kashshaf","surah":113,"ar":"و\"الغاسق\": الليل إذا اعتكر ظلامه، من قوله تعالى: (إِلَى غَسَقِ اللَّيْلِ) [ الإسراء: 78 ]ومنه: غسقت العين امتلأت دمعا، وغسقت الجراحة: امتلأت دمًا.\n\nووقوبه: دخول ظلامه في كل شيء، ويقال: وقبت الشمس إذا غابت. وفي الحديث: لما رأى الشمس قد وقبت قال: هذا حين حلها، يعنى صلاة المغرب. وقيل: هو القمر إذا امتلأ،\n\nوعن عائشة رضي الله عنها: أخذ رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم بيدي فأشار إلى القمر فقال: تعوّذي بالله من شر هذا، فإنه الغاسق إذا وقب، ووقوبه: دخوله في الكسوف واسوداده. ويجوز أن يراد بالغاسق: الأسود من الحيات، ووقبه: ضربه ونقبه. والوقب: النقب، ومنه: وقبة الثريد؛ والتعوّذ من شر الليل؛ لأن انبثاثه فيه أكثر، والتحرّز منه أصعب، ومنه قولهم: الليل أخفى للويل، وقولهم: أغدر الليل؛\n\nلأنه إذا أظلم كثر فيه الغدر، وأسند الشر إليه لملابسته له من حدوثه فيه.","html":"<p>Al-ghāsiq is night when its darkness becomes dense, from His statement “… until the darkness of night” [17:78]. Related expressions are “the eye ghasaqat”—filled with tears—and “the wound ghasaqat”—filled with blood. Its wuqūb (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">وقوب</bdi>) is the entry of its darkness into everything. One says waqabat al-shams when the sun sets. In the report, when he saw that the sun had set, he said, “This is the time when it becomes lawful”—meaning the sunset prayer.\nIt is also said to be the moon when full. ʿĀʾisha—may God be pleased with her—reports: the Messenger of God ﷺ took my hand, pointed to the moon, and said, “Seek refuge in God from the evil of this, for it is the darkening one when it enters.” Its wuqūb (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">وقوب</bdi>) is its entering eclipse and becoming dark.\nAl-ghāsiq may also mean a black snake; its waqb is its striking and piercing. Waqb means a hole, as in waqbat al-tharīd (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الثريد</bdi>), the hollow in a dish of bread soaked in broth.\nRefuge is sought from the evil of night because evil spreads more widely in it and is harder to guard against. Hence their sayings, “Night conceals misfortune better,” and “Night has become treacherous.” When it grows dark, treachery increases. Evil is attributed to night because it accompanies it, occurring within it.\n[TEXT VARIANT K113-C1: Dubai p.646 cites the opening of 17:78, “Establish prayer at the sun&#39;s decline,” rather than the words containing ghasaq. The independent four-volume transcription and Turkish p.1470 give the relevant latter clause, translated above. Both forms are recorded.]</p>","lemma":"","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/final-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true,"display_normalization":"Reversed electronic reference brackets normalized; source text preserved in the dossier."}},{"id":"T113.04","heading":"[T113.04 | XVI 646–647 | on K113.04]","text":"His words, “When its darkness becomes dense.” Al-Jawharī: “Iʿtakara al-ẓalām: it became intermingled, as though part of it returned upon another because of the slowness of its clearing.”\nHis words, “One says waqabat al-shams when it sets.” Al-Rāghib: “Waqb is like a hollow in something; hence waqabat al-shams. Īqāb is its disappearing.”\nHis words, “This is the time when it becomes lawful”: ḥīn (حين) is nominative; in ḥillihā (حِلّها) the ḥāʾ (ح) has kasra (كَسْرة) and the lām (ل) is genitive. Al-Nihāya: “In the report: when he saw that the sun had waqabat, he said, ‘This is the time of its lawfulness.’ Waqabat means ‘set’; the time of its lawfulness is the time when its performance becomes lawful, meaning the sunset prayer. Wuqūb means entry into anything.”","title":"“When its darkness becomes dense”","author":"tibi","on":["K113.04"],"anchor":"K113.04","surah":113,"ar":"قولُه: (إذا اعتكر ظَلامُه)، الجوهري: «اعتكرَ الظلامُ: اختلطَ كأنه كرَّ بعضُه على بعض من بُطْءِ انجلائِه».\n\nقولُه: (ويقال: وَقَبت الشمس، إذا غابت)، الراغب: «الوَقَبُ كالنُقْرةِ في الشئ، ومنه وَقَبتِ الشمس، والإيقابُ: تَغيّبُها»\n\nقولُه: (هذا حينُ حَلِّها)، برفعِ \"حين\"، وكسرِ الحاءِ، وجرِّ اللامِ من \"حلها\". النهاية:\n\n«وفي الحديث: لّما رأى الشمسَ قد وَقَبتْ، قال: هذا حينُ حلِّها؛ وَقَبتْ: غابت. وحينُ حِلِّها: الوقتُ الذي يَحلُّ فيه أداؤها، يعني: صلاة المغرب. والوُقوبُ: الدخولُ في كلِّ شئ».","html":"<p>His words, “When its darkness becomes dense.” Al-Jawharī: “Iʿtakara al-ẓalām: it became intermingled, as though part of it returned upon another because of the slowness of its clearing.”\nHis words, “One says waqabat al-shams when it sets.” Al-Rāghib: “Waqb is like a hollow in something; hence waqabat al-shams. Īqāb is its disappearing.”\nHis words, “This is the time when it becomes lawful”: ḥīn (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">حين</bdi>) is nominative; in ḥillihā (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">حِلّها</bdi>) the ḥāʾ (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ح</bdi>) has kasra (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">كَسْرة</bdi>) and the lām (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ل</bdi>) is genitive. Al-Nihāya: “In the report: when he saw that the sun had waqabat, he said, ‘This is the time of its lawfulness.’ Waqabat means ‘set’; the time of its lawfulness is the time when its performance becomes lawful, meaning the sunset prayer. Wuqūb means entry into anything.”</p>","lemma":"إذا اعتكر ظلامه","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/final-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}},{"id":"T113.05","heading":"[T113.05 | XVI 647 | on K113.04]","text":"His words, “From ʿĀʾisha—may God be pleased with her.” The report was transmitted by Imam Aḥmad and al-Tirmidhī; it does not contain “he took my hand.”\nThe Imam transmits from Ibn Qutayba: “The moon was called ghāsiq (غاسق) because it is eclipsed and darkens: its light departs and it becomes black. Its wuqūb (وقوب) is entering that blackness.” He also says: “It has been established that the moon is not luminous in its own body; it is therefore called ghāsiq (غاسق). Its wuqūb (وقوب) is the dark moon at the end of the month, when its strength is slight and it is at its lowest condition. For that reason sorcerers then occupy themselves with sorcery that produces illness. This accords with the occasion of revelation of the two sūrahs.” God knows best.\nHis words, “Night conceals misfortune better.” Al-Maydānī says: “That is, do what you wish at night, for it conceals your secret better. The first to say this was Sāriya ibn ʿUwaymir ibn ʿAdī al-ʿUqaylī.” Its occasion is mentioned in his book.\nHis words, “Night has become treacherous”: it is said to be like aḥṣada al-zarʿ (أحصد الزرع) [the crop became ready for harvest], that is, the time of its treachery has arrived; it is also said to mean “it has become possessed of treachery.”","title":"“From ʿĀʾisha—may God be pleased with her”","author":"tibi","on":["K113.04"],"anchor":"K113.04","surah":113,"ar":"قولُه: (وعن عائشة-رضي الله عنها)، الحديثُ اخرجَه الإمامُ أحمدُ والترمذيّ، وليسَ فيه: آخذٌ بيدي؛ روى الإمامُ عن ابنِ قتيبة: «إنما سُمي القمرُ غاسقًا، لأنه يُكْسفُ فيغسِق، أي يذهبُ ضوؤُه، ويَسْود، ووقوبُه: دخولُه في ذلك الاسوداد». وقال: «وقد صَحَّ أن القمرَ في جِرْمه غيرُ مستنير فسمّى بالغاسِق لهذا. ووقوبُه المحاقُ في آخر الشهر، لأنه حينئذٍ قليلُ القوةِ وفي غايةِ الرذالة، ولذلك يشتغلُ السحرةُ فيه بالسحر الذي يورثُ التمريض، وهذا مناسبٌ لسبب نزولِ السورتين «، والله أعلم.\n\nقولُه: (الليلُ أخفى للوَيْل)، قالَ الميداني: «أي: افعلْ ما تريدُ ليلًا، فإنه أسْترُ لسِرِّك. وأولُ مَن قال ذلك ساريةُ بنُ عُويْمرِ بنِ عَدِيٍّ العُقَيلي»، وسببُه مذكورٌ في كتابه.\n\nقولُه: (أَغدرَ الليل)، قيل: هو من بابِ أَحصدَ الزّرع، أي حانَ وقتُ غَدْره. وقيل: صارَ ذا غَدْر.","html":"<p>His words, “From ʿĀʾisha—may God be pleased with her.” The report was transmitted by Imam Aḥmad and al-Tirmidhī; it does not contain “he took my hand.”\nThe Imam transmits from Ibn Qutayba: “The moon was called ghāsiq (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">غاسق</bdi>) because it is eclipsed and darkens: its light departs and it becomes black. Its wuqūb (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">وقوب</bdi>) is entering that blackness.” He also says: “It has been established that the moon is not luminous in its own body; it is therefore called ghāsiq (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">غاسق</bdi>). Its wuqūb (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">وقوب</bdi>) is the dark moon at the end of the month, when its strength is slight and it is at its lowest condition. For that reason sorcerers then occupy themselves with sorcery that produces illness. This accords with the occasion of revelation of the two sūrahs.” God knows best.\nHis words, “Night conceals misfortune better.” Al-Maydānī says: “That is, do what you wish at night, for it conceals your secret better. The first to say this was Sāriya ibn ʿUwaymir ibn ʿAdī al-ʿUqaylī.” Its occasion is mentioned in his book.\nHis words, “Night has become treacherous”: it is said to be like aḥṣada al-zarʿ (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أحصد الزرع</bdi>) [the crop became ready for harvest], that is, the time of its treachery has arrived; it is also said to mean “it has become possessed of treachery.”</p>","lemma":"","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/final-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}}],"cairo_notes":[],"verses":[{"key":"113:3","number":3,"ar":"وَمِن شَرِّ غَاسِقٍ إِذَا وَقَبَ","en":"From the evil of the darkness when it is intense"}],"quran_ar":"وَمِن شَرِّ غَاسِقٍ إِذَا وَقَبَ ﴿3﴾","quran_en":"3. From the evil of the darkness when it is intense"},{"id":"K113.05","q":"113:4","s":113,"surah":"al-Falaq","surah_ar":"الفلق","title":"al-Falaq · Al-naffāthāt are women, souls, or groups of female sorcerers who tie knots in threads, blow upon the","cairo":"K IV:820–822 · T XVI:644–651","source_page_first":820,"source_page_last":822,"chunk":"passages-113","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"Text and explanation","html":"<p>Al-naffāthāt are women, souls, or groups of female sorcerers who tie knots in threads, blow upon them and recite incantations. Nafth is blowing with saliva. This has no effect—unless, indeed, something harmful is administered as food, drink or something to smell, or the bewitched person is brought into contact with it in some way. But God—mighty and majestic—may perform an act at that moment as a test by which those steadfast upon truth are distinguished from the Ḥashwiyya and the ignorant common people. The Ḥashwiyya and the rabble attribute it to the women and their blowing; those steadfast in the firm word pay it no attention and attach no weight to it.\nIf you ask, “What, then, does seeking refuge from their evil mean?”, I answer: there are three interpretations. First, refuge is sought from their activity, the practice of sorcery, and their sin in doing it. Second, refuge is sought from their leading people astray through their sorcery and deceiving them by their false practices. Third, refuge is sought from whatever evil God causes to befall someone at the moment of their blowing.\n[TEXT CORRECTION K113-C2: Both electronic Arabic texts have <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">النفخ من ريق</bdi>. The Dubai scan p.648 clearly reads <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">النفخ مع ريق</bdi>, “blowing with saliva”; Turkish p.1470 agrees. The scan governs this translation. Ḥashwiyya is retained as the author&#39;s polemical label, not silently converted into a neutral modern school name.]</p>","author":"kashshaf","source_id":"K113.05"},{"type":"exposition","title":"“By which those steadfast upon truth are distinguished from the Ḥashwiyya”","html":"<p>His words, “By which those steadfast upon truth are distinguished from the Ḥashwiyya.” Al-Intiṣāf: “The Qadariyya deny sorcery, whereas the Book and the Sunna attest its occurrence; the command to seek refuge from it is evidence for it. The Messenger of God ﷺ was bewitched with a comb, combings and the sheath of a male palm&#39;s spathe.”\nI say: we transmitted the report from al-Bukhārī, Muslim and Ibn Māja, from ʿĀʾisha—may God be pleased with her:\n“The Messenger of God ﷺ was bewitched, so that he imagined he had done something when he had not. One day, while with me, he prayed to God repeatedly, then said: ‘Have you realized, ʿĀʾisha, that God has given me an answer concerning what I asked Him?’ I said, ‘What is that, Messenger of God?’ He said: ‘Two men came to me. One sat at my head and the other at my feet. One asked his companion: What ails the man? He answered: He has been bewitched. He asked: Who bewitched him? He answered: Labīd ibn al-Aʿṣam, the Jew of Banū Zurayq. He asked: With what? He answered: With a comb, combings and the sheath of a male palm&#39;s spathe. He asked: Where is it? He answered: In the well of Dhī Arwān.’” The report [continues].\n[TEXT CORRECTION T113-C5: The scan supplies the question to ʿĀʾisha and her answer, absent from the OpenITI transcription. They are retained here.]</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T113.06"},{"type":"exposition","title":"Commentary","html":"<p>Al-Rāghib: “The effect of sorcery upon the Prophet ﷺ did not concern him inasmuch as he was a prophet; it concerned his body inasmuch as he was a human being or mortal, just as he ate, excreted, became angry, desired and fell ill [and recovered, inasmuch as he was human, not inasmuch as he was a prophet]. It would impair prophethood only [if] sorcery were found to affect something pertaining to prophethood. Just so, his being wounded and having his front teeth broken on the day of Uḥud did not impair the protection God guaranteed him in ‘God will protect you from people’ [5:67]. Likewise, the polytheists&#39; gaining control over certain regions within Islam is not taken as invalidating the perfection of Islam stated in ‘Today I have perfected your religion for you’ [5:3].”\nAl-Qāḍī says: “This does not make the unbelievers truthful in calling him bewitched, for they meant that he was mad through sorcery.”\n[SOURCE CHECK T113-U2: The earlier scan report gives <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فيصح من حيث نبي</bdi> and <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أو وجد للسحر تأثير</bdi>. Al-Rāghib&#39;s own tafsīr of 2:102 reads <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ويمرض ويصح من حيث هو بشر لا من حيث هو نبي</bdi> and <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">لو وجد للسحر تأثير</bdi>. The bracketed restoration follows the quoted source and explains the human capacity in which illness and recovery occur. The same source confirms the positive <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">بما يقع</bdi>, already reported from Dubai XVI:649. Ṭībī&#39;s exact damaged wording still requires another witness; no new scan collation is claimed.]</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T113.07"},{"type":"exposition","title":"“The rabble”","html":"<p>Al-Nihāya: “He was bewitched with a comb and combings”: these are the hairs that fall from the head and beard when combed. It is also transmitted as mushāqa (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">مشاقة</bdi>): “what breaks off from silk and flax when cleaned and combed. Mashq is pulling something to lengthen it.” “Juff is the casing of the spathe, the covering over it.”\nHis words, “The rabble”: immature youths and the base multitude.\nHis words, “Scheming women”: he likens their scheming to sorcery. The author of al-Intiṣāf abridged it and then said: “Had anyone other than al-Zamakhsharī offered this interpretation, it would have been counted among the innovations of exegesis.”</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T113.08"}],"types":["exposition","language","theology","reports"],"ar":"النَّفَّاثاتِ: النساء، أو النفوس، أو الجماعات السواحر اللاتي يعقدن عقدًا في خيوط وينفثن عليها ويرقين، والنفث: النفخ مع ريق، ولا تأثير لذلك، اللهم إلا إذا كان ثم إطعام شيء ضار، أو سقيه، أو إشمامه، أو مباشرة المسحور به على بعض الوجوه؛ ولكنّ الله عز وجل قد يفعل عند ذلك فعلًا على سبيل الامتحان الذي يتميز به الثبت على الحق من الحشوية والجهلة من العوام،\n\nفينسبه الحشوية والرعاع إليهنّ وإلى نفثهن، والثابتون بالقول الثابت لا يلتفتون إلى ذلك ولا يعبئون به.\n\nفإن قلت: فما معنى الاستعاذة من شرهن؟\n\nقلت: فيها ثلاثة أوجه، أحدها: أن يستعاذ من عملهن الذي هو صنعة السحر ومن إثمهنّ في ذلك. والثاني: أن يستعاذ من فتنتهن الناس بسحرهن وما يخدعنهم به من باطلهن. والثالث: أن يستعاذ مما يصيب الله به من الشر عند نفثهن.\n\nقوُله: (يَتميز به الثُّبَّتُ على الحقِّ من الحَشْوية)، الانتصاف: «القدريّةُ ينكرونَ السحر، والكتابُ والسُّنةُ واردانِ بوقوعِه، والأمرُ بالتعوّذِ منه دليلٌ عليه. وقد سُحِرَ رسولُ الله صلى الله عليه وسلم، في مُشُطٍ ومُشاطَةٍ وجُفِّ طَلْعةِ ذَكَر».\n\nوقلتُ: الحديثُ رويناه عن البخاريِّ ومسلمٍ وابنِ ماجه، عن عائشةَ رضي الله عنها قالت: «سُحِرَ رسولُ الله صلى الله عليه وسلم، حتى إنه ليُخَيّلُ إليه أنه فعلَ الشئَ ولم يكن فعلَه، حتى إذا كانَ ذاتَ يومٍ وهو عندي، دعا الله ودَعاه، ثم قالَ: [فجوة في النقل الإلكتروني: سؤال النبي لعائشة وجوابها؛ تُراجع صورة الصفحة.] جاءني رجلان، فجلس أحدهُما عند رأسي والآخر عند رِجْليَّ، ثم قال أحدهما لصاحبه: ما وَجَعُ الرجل؟ قال مَطْبوب. قال: ومَن طَبَّه؟ قال: لبيدُ بنُ الأعصمِ اليهوديُّ من بني زُريق. قال: في ماذا؟ قال: في مُشُطٍ ومُشاطةٍ وجُفِّ طَلْعةِ ذَكَرٍ.\n\nقال: فأين هو؟ قال: في بئرِ ذي أَرْوان»، الحديث.\n\nالراغب: «تأثيرُ السحرِ في النبيِّ صلى الله عليه وسلم، لم يكن من حيثُ إنه نبي، وإنما كانَ في بَدَنِه من حيثُ إنه إنسانٌ أو بشر، كما كان يأكلُ ويتغوّطُ ويغضبُ ويَشْتهي ويَمْرض، [ويصح من حيث هو بشر لا من حيث هو نبي]، وإنما يكون ذلك قادحًا في النبوة [لو وُجدَ] للسحرِ تأثيرٌ في أمر يرجعُ إلى النبوة،\n\nكما أن جُرْحَه وكسرَ ثناياه يومَ أُحُد، لم يقدحْ فيما ضمنَ اللهُ له من عصمته في قوله: ﴿وَاللَّهُ يَعْصِمُكَ مِنَ النَّاسِ﴾ [المائدة: 67]، وكما لا اعتدادَ بما يقعُ في الإسلامِ من غلبةِ المشركين على بعضِ النواحي، فيما ذُكرَ من كمالِ الإسلامِ في قوله: ﴿الْيَوْمَ أَكْمَلْتُ لَكُمْ دِينَكُمْ﴾ [المائدة: 3]، قالَ القاضي: «ولا يوجبُ ذلك صِدْقَ الكفرةِ في أنه مسحور، لأنهم أرادوا به أنه مجنونٌ بواسطةِ السحر».\n\nالنهاية: «أنه طُبَّ في مُشُطٍ ومُشاطة، وهو الشعرُ الذي يسقطُ من الرأسِ واللحيةِ عند التسريحِ بالمُشُط». ويُرْوى: مُشاقة، و «هي ما ينقطعُ من الإبْرَيْسَمِ والكَتّانِ عند تخليصِه وتَسْريحه. والمَشْقُ: جَذْبُ الشئِ ليطول». «الجُفّ: وعاءُ الطلع، وهو الغشاءُ الذي يكونُ فوقه».\n\nقولُه: (الِّرعَاع)، الأحداثُ والطَّغام.\n\nقولُه: (النساءُ الكيّادات)، شُبِّه كيدُهنّ بالسحر، اختصَره صاحبُ \"الانتصاف\" ثُم قال: «لو فَسَّرَ غيرُ الزمخشريّ هذا، لَعُدَّ من بِدَعِ التفاسير».","layers":[{"id":"K113.05","heading":"[K113.05 | T XVI 648–649; K IV 821]","text":"Al-naffāthāt are women, souls, or groups of female sorcerers who tie knots in threads, blow upon them and recite incantations. Nafth is blowing with saliva. This has no effect—unless, indeed, something harmful is administered as food, drink or something to smell, or the bewitched person is brought into contact with it in some way. But God—mighty and majestic—may perform an act at that moment as a test by which those steadfast upon truth are distinguished from the Ḥashwiyya and the ignorant common people. The Ḥashwiyya and the rabble attribute it to the women and their blowing; those steadfast in the firm word pay it no attention and attach no weight to it.\nIf you ask, “What, then, does seeking refuge from their evil mean?”, I answer: there are three interpretations. First, refuge is sought from their activity, the practice of sorcery, and their sin in doing it. Second, refuge is sought from their leading people astray through their sorcery and deceiving them by their false practices. Third, refuge is sought from whatever evil God causes to befall someone at the moment of their blowing.\n[TEXT CORRECTION K113-C2: Both electronic Arabic texts have النفخ من ريق. The Dubai scan p.648 clearly reads النفخ مع ريق, “blowing with saliva”; Turkish p.1470 agrees. The scan governs this translation. Ḥashwiyya is retained as the author's polemical label, not silently converted into a neutral modern school name.]","title":"Text and explanation","author":"kashshaf","surah":113,"ar":"النَّفَّاثاتِ: النساء، أو النفوس، أو الجماعات السواحر اللاتي يعقدن عقدًا في خيوط وينفثن عليها ويرقين، والنفث: النفخ مع ريق، ولا تأثير لذلك، اللهم إلا إذا كان ثم إطعام شيء ضار، أو سقيه، أو إشمامه، أو مباشرة المسحور به على بعض الوجوه؛ ولكنّ الله عز وجل قد يفعل عند ذلك فعلًا على سبيل الامتحان الذي يتميز به الثبت على الحق من الحشوية والجهلة من العوام،\n\nفينسبه الحشوية والرعاع إليهنّ وإلى نفثهن، والثابتون بالقول الثابت لا يلتفتون إلى ذلك ولا يعبئون به.\n\nفإن قلت: فما معنى الاستعاذة من شرهن؟\n\nقلت: فيها ثلاثة أوجه، أحدها: أن يستعاذ من عملهن الذي هو صنعة السحر ومن إثمهنّ في ذلك. والثاني: أن يستعاذ من فتنتهن الناس بسحرهن وما يخدعنهم به من باطلهن. والثالث: أن يستعاذ مما يصيب الله به من الشر عند نفثهن.","html":"<p>Al-naffāthāt are women, souls, or groups of female sorcerers who tie knots in threads, blow upon them and recite incantations. Nafth is blowing with saliva. This has no effect—unless, indeed, something harmful is administered as food, drink or something to smell, or the bewitched person is brought into contact with it in some way. But God—mighty and majestic—may perform an act at that moment as a test by which those steadfast upon truth are distinguished from the Ḥashwiyya and the ignorant common people. The Ḥashwiyya and the rabble attribute it to the women and their blowing; those steadfast in the firm word pay it no attention and attach no weight to it.\nIf you ask, “What, then, does seeking refuge from their evil mean?”, I answer: there are three interpretations. First, refuge is sought from their activity, the practice of sorcery, and their sin in doing it. Second, refuge is sought from their leading people astray through their sorcery and deceiving them by their false practices. Third, refuge is sought from whatever evil God causes to befall someone at the moment of their blowing.\n[TEXT CORRECTION K113-C2: Both electronic Arabic texts have <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">النفخ من ريق</bdi>. The Dubai scan p.648 clearly reads <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">النفخ مع ريق</bdi>, “blowing with saliva”; Turkish p.1470 agrees. The scan governs this translation. Ḥashwiyya is retained as the author&#39;s polemical label, not silently converted into a neutral modern school name.]</p>","lemma":"","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/final-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}},{"id":"T113.06","heading":"[T113.06 | XVI 648 | on K113.05]","text":"His words, “By which those steadfast upon truth are distinguished from the Ḥashwiyya.” Al-Intiṣāf: “The Qadariyya deny sorcery, whereas the Book and the Sunna attest its occurrence; the command to seek refuge from it is evidence for it. The Messenger of God ﷺ was bewitched with a comb, combings and the sheath of a male palm's spathe.”\nI say: we transmitted the report from al-Bukhārī, Muslim and Ibn Māja, from ʿĀʾisha—may God be pleased with her:\n“The Messenger of God ﷺ was bewitched, so that he imagined he had done something when he had not. One day, while with me, he prayed to God repeatedly, then said: ‘Have you realized, ʿĀʾisha, that God has given me an answer concerning what I asked Him?’ I said, ‘What is that, Messenger of God?’ He said: ‘Two men came to me. One sat at my head and the other at my feet. One asked his companion: What ails the man? He answered: He has been bewitched. He asked: Who bewitched him? He answered: Labīd ibn al-Aʿṣam, the Jew of Banū Zurayq. He asked: With what? He answered: With a comb, combings and the sheath of a male palm's spathe. He asked: Where is it? He answered: In the well of Dhī Arwān.’” The report [continues].\n[TEXT CORRECTION T113-C5: The scan supplies the question to ʿĀʾisha and her answer, absent from the OpenITI transcription. They are retained here.]","title":"“By which those steadfast upon truth are distinguished from the Ḥashwiyya”","author":"tibi","on":["K113.05"],"anchor":"K113.05","surah":113,"ar":"قوُله: (يَتميز به الثُّبَّتُ على الحقِّ من الحَشْوية)، الانتصاف: «القدريّةُ ينكرونَ السحر، والكتابُ والسُّنةُ واردانِ بوقوعِه، والأمرُ بالتعوّذِ منه دليلٌ عليه. وقد سُحِرَ رسولُ الله صلى الله عليه وسلم، في مُشُطٍ ومُشاطَةٍ وجُفِّ طَلْعةِ ذَكَر».\n\nوقلتُ: الحديثُ رويناه عن البخاريِّ ومسلمٍ وابنِ ماجه، عن عائشةَ رضي الله عنها قالت: «سُحِرَ رسولُ الله صلى الله عليه وسلم، حتى إنه ليُخَيّلُ إليه أنه فعلَ الشئَ ولم يكن فعلَه، حتى إذا كانَ ذاتَ يومٍ وهو عندي، دعا الله ودَعاه، ثم قالَ: [فجوة في النقل الإلكتروني: سؤال النبي لعائشة وجوابها؛ تُراجع صورة الصفحة.] جاءني رجلان، فجلس أحدهُما عند رأسي والآخر عند رِجْليَّ، ثم قال أحدهما لصاحبه: ما وَجَعُ الرجل؟ قال مَطْبوب. قال: ومَن طَبَّه؟ قال: لبيدُ بنُ الأعصمِ اليهوديُّ من بني زُريق. قال: في ماذا؟ قال: في مُشُطٍ ومُشاطةٍ وجُفِّ طَلْعةِ ذَكَرٍ.\n\nقال: فأين هو؟ قال: في بئرِ ذي أَرْوان»، الحديث.","html":"<p>His words, “By which those steadfast upon truth are distinguished from the Ḥashwiyya.” Al-Intiṣāf: “The Qadariyya deny sorcery, whereas the Book and the Sunna attest its occurrence; the command to seek refuge from it is evidence for it. The Messenger of God ﷺ was bewitched with a comb, combings and the sheath of a male palm&#39;s spathe.”\nI say: we transmitted the report from al-Bukhārī, Muslim and Ibn Māja, from ʿĀʾisha—may God be pleased with her:\n“The Messenger of God ﷺ was bewitched, so that he imagined he had done something when he had not. One day, while with me, he prayed to God repeatedly, then said: ‘Have you realized, ʿĀʾisha, that God has given me an answer concerning what I asked Him?’ I said, ‘What is that, Messenger of God?’ He said: ‘Two men came to me. One sat at my head and the other at my feet. One asked his companion: What ails the man? He answered: He has been bewitched. He asked: Who bewitched him? He answered: Labīd ibn al-Aʿṣam, the Jew of Banū Zurayq. He asked: With what? He answered: With a comb, combings and the sheath of a male palm&#39;s spathe. He asked: Where is it? He answered: In the well of Dhī Arwān.’” The report [continues].\n[TEXT CORRECTION T113-C5: The scan supplies the question to ʿĀʾisha and her answer, absent from the OpenITI transcription. They are retained here.]</p>","lemma":"يتميز به الثبت على الحق من الحشوية","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/final-surahs.json","arabic_complete":false}},{"id":"T113.07","heading":"[T113.07 | XVI 648–649 | continuation]","text":"Al-Rāghib: “The effect of sorcery upon the Prophet ﷺ did not concern him inasmuch as he was a prophet; it concerned his body inasmuch as he was a human being or mortal, just as he ate, excreted, became angry, desired and fell ill [and recovered, inasmuch as he was human, not inasmuch as he was a prophet]. It would impair prophethood only [if] sorcery were found to affect something pertaining to prophethood. Just so, his being wounded and having his front teeth broken on the day of Uḥud did not impair the protection God guaranteed him in ‘God will protect you from people’ [5:67]. Likewise, the polytheists' gaining control over certain regions within Islam is not taken as invalidating the perfection of Islam stated in ‘Today I have perfected your religion for you’ [5:3].”\nAl-Qāḍī says: “This does not make the unbelievers truthful in calling him bewitched, for they meant that he was mad through sorcery.”\n[SOURCE CHECK T113-U2: The earlier scan report gives فيصح من حيث نبي and أو وجد للسحر تأثير. Al-Rāghib's own tafsīr of 2:102 reads ويمرض ويصح من حيث هو بشر لا من حيث هو نبي and لو وجد للسحر تأثير. The bracketed restoration follows the quoted source and explains the human capacity in which illness and recovery occur. The same source confirms the positive بما يقع, already reported from Dubai XVI:649. Ṭībī's exact damaged wording still requires another witness; no new scan collation is claimed.]","title":"Commentary","author":"tibi","on":["K113.05"],"anchor":"K113.05","surah":113,"ar":"الراغب: «تأثيرُ السحرِ في النبيِّ صلى الله عليه وسلم، لم يكن من حيثُ إنه نبي، وإنما كانَ في بَدَنِه من حيثُ إنه إنسانٌ أو بشر، كما كان يأكلُ ويتغوّطُ ويغضبُ ويَشْتهي ويَمْرض، [ويصح من حيث هو بشر لا من حيث هو نبي]، وإنما يكون ذلك قادحًا في النبوة [لو وُجدَ] للسحرِ تأثيرٌ في أمر يرجعُ إلى النبوة،\n\nكما أن جُرْحَه وكسرَ ثناياه يومَ أُحُد، لم يقدحْ فيما ضمنَ اللهُ له من عصمته في قوله: ﴿وَاللَّهُ يَعْصِمُكَ مِنَ النَّاسِ﴾ [المائدة: 67]، وكما لا اعتدادَ بما يقعُ في الإسلامِ من غلبةِ المشركين على بعضِ النواحي، فيما ذُكرَ من كمالِ الإسلامِ في قوله: ﴿الْيَوْمَ أَكْمَلْتُ لَكُمْ دِينَكُمْ﴾ [المائدة: 3]، قالَ القاضي: «ولا يوجبُ ذلك صِدْقَ الكفرةِ في أنه مسحور، لأنهم أرادوا به أنه مجنونٌ بواسطةِ السحر».","html":"<p>Al-Rāghib: “The effect of sorcery upon the Prophet ﷺ did not concern him inasmuch as he was a prophet; it concerned his body inasmuch as he was a human being or mortal, just as he ate, excreted, became angry, desired and fell ill [and recovered, inasmuch as he was human, not inasmuch as he was a prophet]. It would impair prophethood only [if] sorcery were found to affect something pertaining to prophethood. Just so, his being wounded and having his front teeth broken on the day of Uḥud did not impair the protection God guaranteed him in ‘God will protect you from people’ [5:67]. Likewise, the polytheists&#39; gaining control over certain regions within Islam is not taken as invalidating the perfection of Islam stated in ‘Today I have perfected your religion for you’ [5:3].”\nAl-Qāḍī says: “This does not make the unbelievers truthful in calling him bewitched, for they meant that he was mad through sorcery.”\n[SOURCE CHECK T113-U2: The earlier scan report gives <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">فيصح من حيث نبي</bdi> and <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أو وجد للسحر تأثير</bdi>. Al-Rāghib&#39;s own tafsīr of 2:102 reads <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ويمرض ويصح من حيث هو بشر لا من حيث هو نبي</bdi> and <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">لو وجد للسحر تأثير</bdi>. The bracketed restoration follows the quoted source and explains the human capacity in which illness and recovery occur. The same source confirms the positive <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">بما يقع</bdi>, already reported from Dubai XVI:649. Ṭībī&#39;s exact damaged wording still requires another witness; no new scan collation is claimed.]</p>","lemma":"والله يعصمك من الناس","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/final-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}},{"id":"T113.08","heading":"[T113.08 | XVI 649 | continuation]","text":"Al-Nihāya: “He was bewitched with a comb and combings”: these are the hairs that fall from the head and beard when combed. It is also transmitted as mushāqa (مشاقة): “what breaks off from silk and flax when cleaned and combed. Mashq is pulling something to lengthen it.” “Juff is the casing of the spathe, the covering over it.”\nHis words, “The rabble”: immature youths and the base multitude.\nHis words, “Scheming women”: he likens their scheming to sorcery. The author of al-Intiṣāf abridged it and then said: “Had anyone other than al-Zamakhsharī offered this interpretation, it would have been counted among the innovations of exegesis.”","title":"“The rabble”","author":"tibi","on":["K113.05"],"anchor":"K113.05","surah":113,"ar":"النهاية: «أنه طُبَّ في مُشُطٍ ومُشاطة، وهو الشعرُ الذي يسقطُ من الرأسِ واللحيةِ عند التسريحِ بالمُشُط». ويُرْوى: مُشاقة، و «هي ما ينقطعُ من الإبْرَيْسَمِ والكَتّانِ عند تخليصِه وتَسْريحه. والمَشْقُ: جَذْبُ الشئِ ليطول». «الجُفّ: وعاءُ الطلع، وهو الغشاءُ الذي يكونُ فوقه».\n\nقولُه: (الِّرعَاع)، الأحداثُ والطَّغام.\n\nقولُه: (النساءُ الكيّادات)، شُبِّه كيدُهنّ بالسحر، اختصَره صاحبُ \"الانتصاف\" ثُم قال: «لو فَسَّرَ غيرُ الزمخشريّ هذا، لَعُدَّ من بِدَعِ التفاسير».","html":"<p>Al-Nihāya: “He was bewitched with a comb and combings”: these are the hairs that fall from the head and beard when combed. It is also transmitted as mushāqa (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">مشاقة</bdi>): “what breaks off from silk and flax when cleaned and combed. Mashq is pulling something to lengthen it.” “Juff is the casing of the spathe, the covering over it.”\nHis words, “The rabble”: immature youths and the base multitude.\nHis words, “Scheming women”: he likens their scheming to sorcery. The author of al-Intiṣāf abridged it and then said: “Had anyone other than al-Zamakhsharī offered this interpretation, it would have been counted among the innovations of exegesis.”</p>","lemma":"الرعاع","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/final-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}}],"cairo_notes":[],"verses":[{"key":"113:4","number":4,"ar":"وَمِن شَرِّ ٱلنَّفَّـٰثَـٰتِ فِى ٱلْعُقَدِ","en":"And from the evil of malignant witchcraft"}],"quran_ar":"وَمِن شَرِّ ٱلنَّفَّـٰثَـٰتِ فِى ٱلْعُقَدِ ﴿4﴾","quran_en":"4. And from the evil of malignant witchcraft"},{"id":"K113.06","q":"113:4–5","s":113,"surah":"al-Falaq","surah_ar":"الفلق","title":"al-Falaq · They may also mean scheming women, from His statement “Your scheming is indeed great” [12:28], liken","cairo":"K IV:820–822 · T XVI:644–651","source_page_first":820,"source_page_last":822,"chunk":"passages-113","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"Text and explanation","html":"<p>They may also mean scheming women, from His statement “Your scheming is indeed great” [12:28], likening their scheming to sorcery and blowing on knots; or women who tempt men by presenting themselves to them and displaying their attractions, as though bewitching them through that.\n“When he envies”: when his envy becomes manifest and he acts as it dictates, seeking misfortunes for the person envied. If he does not manifest the effect of what he conceals, no harm from him reaches the person he envies. Rather, he harms himself by grieving at another&#39;s happiness. ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz is reported to have said: “I have never seen a wrongdoer who more resembles someone wronged than an envier.”\nThe envier&#39;s evil may also mean his sin and the ugliness of his condition at the time of his envy and his displaying its effect.</p>","author":"kashshaf","source_id":"K113.06"}],"types":["exposition","language","theology","reports"],"ar":"ويجوز أن يراد بهن النساء الكيادات،\n\nمن قوله: (إِنَّ كَيْدَكُنَّ عَظِيمٌ) [ يوسف: 28 ]تشبيهًا لكيدهن بالسحر والنفث في العقد. أو اللاتي يفتن الرجال بتعرّضهن لهم وعرضهن محاسنهن، كأنهن يسحرنهم بذلك (إِذا حَسَدَ) إذا ظهر حسده، وعمل بمقتضاه من بغى الغوائل للمحسود؛ لأنه إذا لم يظهر أثر ما أضمره فلا ضرر يعود منه على من حسده، بل هو الضارّ لنفسه لاغتمامه بسرور غيره. وعن عمر بن عبد العزيز: لم أر ظالما أشبه بالمظلوم من حاسد. ويجوز أن يراد بشر الحاسد: إثمه وسماجة حاله في وقت حسده، وإظهاره أثره.","layers":[{"id":"K113.06","heading":"[K113.06 | T XVI 649–650; K IV 821–822]","text":"They may also mean scheming women, from His statement “Your scheming is indeed great” [12:28], likening their scheming to sorcery and blowing on knots; or women who tempt men by presenting themselves to them and displaying their attractions, as though bewitching them through that.\n“When he envies”: when his envy becomes manifest and he acts as it dictates, seeking misfortunes for the person envied. If he does not manifest the effect of what he conceals, no harm from him reaches the person he envies. Rather, he harms himself by grieving at another's happiness. ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz is reported to have said: “I have never seen a wrongdoer who more resembles someone wronged than an envier.”\nThe envier's evil may also mean his sin and the ugliness of his condition at the time of his envy and his displaying its effect.","title":"Text and explanation","author":"kashshaf","surah":113,"ar":"ويجوز أن يراد بهن النساء الكيادات،\n\nمن قوله: (إِنَّ كَيْدَكُنَّ عَظِيمٌ) [ يوسف: 28 ]تشبيهًا لكيدهن بالسحر والنفث في العقد. أو اللاتي يفتن الرجال بتعرّضهن لهم وعرضهن محاسنهن، كأنهن يسحرنهم بذلك (إِذا حَسَدَ) إذا ظهر حسده، وعمل بمقتضاه من بغى الغوائل للمحسود؛ لأنه إذا لم يظهر أثر ما أضمره فلا ضرر يعود منه على من حسده، بل هو الضارّ لنفسه لاغتمامه بسرور غيره. وعن عمر بن عبد العزيز: لم أر ظالما أشبه بالمظلوم من حاسد. ويجوز أن يراد بشر الحاسد: إثمه وسماجة حاله في وقت حسده، وإظهاره أثره.","html":"<p>They may also mean scheming women, from His statement “Your scheming is indeed great” [12:28], likening their scheming to sorcery and blowing on knots; or women who tempt men by presenting themselves to them and displaying their attractions, as though bewitching them through that.\n“When he envies”: when his envy becomes manifest and he acts as it dictates, seeking misfortunes for the person envied. If he does not manifest the effect of what he conceals, no harm from him reaches the person he envies. Rather, he harms himself by grieving at another&#39;s happiness. ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz is reported to have said: “I have never seen a wrongdoer who more resembles someone wronged than an envier.”\nThe envier&#39;s evil may also mean his sin and the ugliness of his condition at the time of his envy and his displaying its effect.</p>","lemma":"","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/final-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true,"display_normalization":"Reversed electronic reference brackets normalized; source text preserved in the dossier."}}],"cairo_notes":[],"verses":[{"key":"113:4","number":4,"ar":"وَمِن شَرِّ ٱلنَّفَّـٰثَـٰتِ فِى ٱلْعُقَدِ","en":"And from the evil of malignant witchcraft"},{"key":"113:5","number":5,"ar":"وَمِن شَرِّ حَاسِدٍ إِذَا حَسَدَ","en":"And from the evil of the envier when he envieth"}],"quran_ar":"وَمِن شَرِّ ٱلنَّفَّـٰثَـٰتِ فِى ٱلْعُقَدِ ﴿4﴾ وَمِن شَرِّ حَاسِدٍ إِذَا حَسَدَ ﴿5﴾","quran_en":"4. And from the evil of malignant witchcraft 5. And from the evil of the envier when he envieth"},{"id":"K113.07","q":"113:1–5","s":113,"surah":"al-Falaq","surah_ar":"الفلق","title":"al-Falaq · If you ask, “‘From the evil of what He created’ includes everything from which refuge is sought","cairo":"K IV:820–822 · T XVI:644–651","source_page_first":820,"source_page_last":822,"chunk":"passages-113","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"Text and explanation","html":"<p>If you ask, “‘From the evil of what He created’ includes everything from which refuge is sought. What is the point, then, of subsequently seeking refuge from the darkening one, the women who blow and the envier?”, I answer: their evil is singled out from all evil because its operation is hidden and reaches a person from where he does not know, as though he were treacherously struck by it. They say: “The worst enemy is the one who conceals his hostility and plots against you without your perceiving it.”\nIf you ask, “Why are some of those from whom refuge is sought made definite and others indefinite?”, I answer: al-naffāthāt (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">النفّاثات</bdi>) is made definite because every such blower is evil. Ghāsiq is indefinite because evil is not present in every darkening night, but in some and not others. Likewise, not every envier causes harm. There is even praiseworthy envy: envy in good things. Hence his statement—blessings and peace be upon him—“There is no envy except in two cases.”\nAbū Tammām said:\n“One who envies noble accomplishments is no [blameworthy] envier.”\nHe also said:\n“Loftiness is such that envy of its like is fair.”\nIt is transmitted from the Messenger of God ﷺ: “Whoever recites the two sūrahs of refuge (al-Muʿawwidhatān) is as though he had recited all the books God Most High has sent down.”\n[TEXT VARIANT K113-C3: Dubai p.650 omits <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">شر العداة</bdi>, “the worst enemy,” before <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">المداجي</bdi>. It is present in the independent four-volume transcription and the Turkish Arabic/Turkish passage; it is included here and the difference recorded.]</p>","author":"kashshaf","source_id":"K113.07"},{"type":"exposition","title":"“As though he were treacherously struck by it”","html":"<p>His words, “As though he were treacherously struck by it.” Al-Asās: “So-and-so treacherously attacks whoever passes him; he killed him by stealth; I fear his ghāʾila (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">غائلة</bdi>), that is, the eventual consequence of his evil.”\nHis words, “There is no envy except in two cases.” We transmitted from al-Bukhārī, from Abū Hurayra, that the Messenger of God ﷺ said: “There is no envy except concerning two: a man to whom God has given the Qurʾān, who recites it at times throughout night and day. His neighbour hears him and says, ‘Would that I had been given what this man was given, so that I might do as he does!’ And a man to whom God has given wealth, who spends it as it should be spent. Someone says, ‘Would that I had been given what this man was given, so that I might do as he does!’”\nAl-Nihāya: “Ḥasad is that a man sees a blessing enjoyed by his brother and wishes it would leave him and become his own instead. Ghabṭ is to wish to have its like without wishing it to leave him. The report means: there is no harmless envy except in two cases.”</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T113.09"},{"type":"exposition","title":"“One who envies …”","html":"<p>His words, “One who envies …”: its opening is:\n“I am indeed envied, and I excuse my envier.”\nIt is also said that its opening is:\n“They envied him his glory—without being blameworthy;\none who envies noble accomplishments is no [blameworthy] envier.”\nHe also said:\n“Excuse your envier for what has been bestowed especially upon you:\nloftiness is such that envy of its like is fair.”\nMithl [like] here is like what occurs in your saying “[his like] is generous”: that is, loftiness itself is something of which envy is fair.\n[REVIEW T113-U3: The scan&#39;s grammatical example is truncated: <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">مثل هاهنا مثل ما في قولك</bdi>: <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">يجود</bdi>. The bracketed words supply its apparent point; check the quoted poetic/grammatical context.]\nThe sūrah is complete.</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T113.10"}],"types":["exposition","language","theology","reports"],"ar":"فإن قلت: قوله: (مِنْ شَرِّ ما خَلَقَ) تعميم في كل ما يستعاذ منه، فما معنى الاستعاذة بعده من الغاسق والنفاثات والحاسد؟\n\nقلت: قد خص شر هؤلاء من كل شر لخفاء أمره، وأنه يلحق الإنسان من حيث لا يعلم، كأنما يغتال به. وقالوا: شر العداة المداجي الذي يكيدك من حيث لا تشعر.\n\nفإن قلت: فلم عرف بعض المستعاذ منه ونكر بعضه؟ قلت: عرفت النفاثات؛ لأن كل نفاثة شريرة، ونكر غاسق؛ لأنّ كل غاسق لا يكون فيه الشر، إنما يكون في بعض دون بعض، وكذلك كل حاسد لا يضرّ. ورب حسد محمود، وهو الحسد في الخيرات. ومنه قوله عليه الصلاة والسلام: «لا حسد إلا في اثنتين»،\n\nوقال أبو تمام:\n\nوما حاسد في المكرمات بحاسد\n\nوقال:\n\nإنّ العلا حسن في مثلها الحسد\n\nعن رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم: «من قرأ \"المعوّذتين\"، فكأنما قرأ الكتب التي أنزلها الله تعالى كلها».\n\nقولُه: (كأنما يُغتالُ به)، الأساس: «فلانٌ يَغتالُ مَن يَمرُّ به، وقتَلَه غيلةً، وأخافُ غائلتَه، أي: عاقبةَ شَرِّه».\n\nقولُه: (لا حَسَدَ إلّا في اثنتين)، روينا عن البخاري، عن أبي هريرة، أن رسولَ الله صلى الله عليه وسلم قال: «لا حَسَدَ إلّا على اثنتين: رجلٌ آتاه اللهُ القرآن، فهو يتلوه آناءَ الليلِ والنهار، فسمعَه جارُه فقال: ليتني أوتيتُ مثل ما أوتي فلان، فعملتُ مثل ما يعمل. ورجلٌ آتاه الله مالًا فهو ينفقه في حقه، فقال: يا ليتني أوتيتُ مثل ما أوتي فلان، فعملتُ مثل ما يعمل».\n\nالنهاية: «الحسَدُ: أن يرى الرجلُ لأخيه نعمةٌ، فيتمنّى أن تزول عنه، فتكون له دونه. والغَبْط: أن يتمنى أن يكون له مثلها، ولا يتمنّى زوالها عنه. ومعنى الحديث: ليسَ حسدٌ لا يضرُّ إلّا في اثنتين».\n\nقولُه: (وما حاسدٌ) أولُه:\n\nوإني لمحسودٌ وأعذرُ حاسدي\n\nوقيل: أوله:\n\nهُمُ حَسَدوه -لا ملومين- مَجْدَه وما حاسدٌ في المكرمات بحاسد\n\nوقال:\n\nواعْذِرْ حَسودَك فيما قد خُصِصْتَ به إنّ العُلا حَسَنٌ في مثلِها الحسّدُ\n\nمِثْلُ ها هنا مثلُ ما في قولك: يجود. أي: إن العُلا حَسَنٌ فيها الحسَد.\n\nتَمَّتِ السُّورَة","layers":[{"id":"K113.07","heading":"[K113.07 | T XVI 650–651; K IV 822]","text":"If you ask, “‘From the evil of what He created’ includes everything from which refuge is sought. What is the point, then, of subsequently seeking refuge from the darkening one, the women who blow and the envier?”, I answer: their evil is singled out from all evil because its operation is hidden and reaches a person from where he does not know, as though he were treacherously struck by it. They say: “The worst enemy is the one who conceals his hostility and plots against you without your perceiving it.”\nIf you ask, “Why are some of those from whom refuge is sought made definite and others indefinite?”, I answer: al-naffāthāt (النفّاثات) is made definite because every such blower is evil. Ghāsiq is indefinite because evil is not present in every darkening night, but in some and not others. Likewise, not every envier causes harm. There is even praiseworthy envy: envy in good things. Hence his statement—blessings and peace be upon him—“There is no envy except in two cases.”\nAbū Tammām said:\n“One who envies noble accomplishments is no [blameworthy] envier.”\nHe also said:\n“Loftiness is such that envy of its like is fair.”\nIt is transmitted from the Messenger of God ﷺ: “Whoever recites the two sūrahs of refuge (al-Muʿawwidhatān) is as though he had recited all the books God Most High has sent down.”\n[TEXT VARIANT K113-C3: Dubai p.650 omits شر العداة, “the worst enemy,” before المداجي. It is present in the independent four-volume transcription and the Turkish Arabic/Turkish passage; it is included here and the difference recorded.]","title":"Text and explanation","author":"kashshaf","surah":113,"ar":"فإن قلت: قوله: (مِنْ شَرِّ ما خَلَقَ) تعميم في كل ما يستعاذ منه، فما معنى الاستعاذة بعده من الغاسق والنفاثات والحاسد؟\n\nقلت: قد خص شر هؤلاء من كل شر لخفاء أمره، وأنه يلحق الإنسان من حيث لا يعلم، كأنما يغتال به. وقالوا: شر العداة المداجي الذي يكيدك من حيث لا تشعر.\n\nفإن قلت: فلم عرف بعض المستعاذ منه ونكر بعضه؟ قلت: عرفت النفاثات؛ لأن كل نفاثة شريرة، ونكر غاسق؛ لأنّ كل غاسق لا يكون فيه الشر، إنما يكون في بعض دون بعض، وكذلك كل حاسد لا يضرّ. ورب حسد محمود، وهو الحسد في الخيرات. ومنه قوله عليه الصلاة والسلام: «لا حسد إلا في اثنتين»،\n\nوقال أبو تمام:\n\nوما حاسد في المكرمات بحاسد\n\nوقال:\n\nإنّ العلا حسن في مثلها الحسد\n\nعن رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم: «من قرأ \"المعوّذتين\"، فكأنما قرأ الكتب التي أنزلها الله تعالى كلها».","html":"<p>If you ask, “‘From the evil of what He created’ includes everything from which refuge is sought. What is the point, then, of subsequently seeking refuge from the darkening one, the women who blow and the envier?”, I answer: their evil is singled out from all evil because its operation is hidden and reaches a person from where he does not know, as though he were treacherously struck by it. They say: “The worst enemy is the one who conceals his hostility and plots against you without your perceiving it.”\nIf you ask, “Why are some of those from whom refuge is sought made definite and others indefinite?”, I answer: al-naffāthāt (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">النفّاثات</bdi>) is made definite because every such blower is evil. Ghāsiq is indefinite because evil is not present in every darkening night, but in some and not others. Likewise, not every envier causes harm. There is even praiseworthy envy: envy in good things. Hence his statement—blessings and peace be upon him—“There is no envy except in two cases.”\nAbū Tammām said:\n“One who envies noble accomplishments is no [blameworthy] envier.”\nHe also said:\n“Loftiness is such that envy of its like is fair.”\nIt is transmitted from the Messenger of God ﷺ: “Whoever recites the two sūrahs of refuge (al-Muʿawwidhatān) is as though he had recited all the books God Most High has sent down.”\n[TEXT VARIANT K113-C3: Dubai p.650 omits <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">شر العداة</bdi>, “the worst enemy,” before <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">المداجي</bdi>. It is present in the independent four-volume transcription and the Turkish Arabic/Turkish passage; it is included here and the difference recorded.]</p>","lemma":"","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/final-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}},{"id":"T113.09","heading":"[T113.09 | XVI 650–651 | on K113.07]","text":"His words, “As though he were treacherously struck by it.” Al-Asās: “So-and-so treacherously attacks whoever passes him; he killed him by stealth; I fear his ghāʾila (غائلة), that is, the eventual consequence of his evil.”\nHis words, “There is no envy except in two cases.” We transmitted from al-Bukhārī, from Abū Hurayra, that the Messenger of God ﷺ said: “There is no envy except concerning two: a man to whom God has given the Qurʾān, who recites it at times throughout night and day. His neighbour hears him and says, ‘Would that I had been given what this man was given, so that I might do as he does!’ And a man to whom God has given wealth, who spends it as it should be spent. Someone says, ‘Would that I had been given what this man was given, so that I might do as he does!’”\nAl-Nihāya: “Ḥasad is that a man sees a blessing enjoyed by his brother and wishes it would leave him and become his own instead. Ghabṭ is to wish to have its like without wishing it to leave him. The report means: there is no harmless envy except in two cases.”","title":"“As though he were treacherously struck by it”","author":"tibi","on":["K113.07"],"anchor":"K113.07","surah":113,"ar":"قولُه: (كأنما يُغتالُ به)، الأساس: «فلانٌ يَغتالُ مَن يَمرُّ به، وقتَلَه غيلةً، وأخافُ غائلتَه، أي: عاقبةَ شَرِّه».\n\nقولُه: (لا حَسَدَ إلّا في اثنتين)، روينا عن البخاري، عن أبي هريرة، أن رسولَ الله صلى الله عليه وسلم قال: «لا حَسَدَ إلّا على اثنتين: رجلٌ آتاه اللهُ القرآن، فهو يتلوه آناءَ الليلِ والنهار، فسمعَه جارُه فقال: ليتني أوتيتُ مثل ما أوتي فلان، فعملتُ مثل ما يعمل. ورجلٌ آتاه الله مالًا فهو ينفقه في حقه، فقال: يا ليتني أوتيتُ مثل ما أوتي فلان، فعملتُ مثل ما يعمل».\n\nالنهاية: «الحسَدُ: أن يرى الرجلُ لأخيه نعمةٌ، فيتمنّى أن تزول عنه، فتكون له دونه. والغَبْط: أن يتمنى أن يكون له مثلها، ولا يتمنّى زوالها عنه. ومعنى الحديث: ليسَ حسدٌ لا يضرُّ إلّا في اثنتين».","html":"<p>His words, “As though he were treacherously struck by it.” Al-Asās: “So-and-so treacherously attacks whoever passes him; he killed him by stealth; I fear his ghāʾila (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">غائلة</bdi>), that is, the eventual consequence of his evil.”\nHis words, “There is no envy except in two cases.” We transmitted from al-Bukhārī, from Abū Hurayra, that the Messenger of God ﷺ said: “There is no envy except concerning two: a man to whom God has given the Qurʾān, who recites it at times throughout night and day. His neighbour hears him and says, ‘Would that I had been given what this man was given, so that I might do as he does!’ And a man to whom God has given wealth, who spends it as it should be spent. Someone says, ‘Would that I had been given what this man was given, so that I might do as he does!’”\nAl-Nihāya: “Ḥasad is that a man sees a blessing enjoyed by his brother and wishes it would leave him and become his own instead. Ghabṭ is to wish to have its like without wishing it to leave him. The report means: there is no harmless envy except in two cases.”</p>","lemma":"كأنما يغتال به","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/final-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}},{"id":"T113.10","heading":"[T113.10 | XVI 651 | on K113.07]","text":"His words, “One who envies …”: its opening is:\n“I am indeed envied, and I excuse my envier.”\nIt is also said that its opening is:\n“They envied him his glory—without being blameworthy;\none who envies noble accomplishments is no [blameworthy] envier.”\nHe also said:\n“Excuse your envier for what has been bestowed especially upon you:\nloftiness is such that envy of its like is fair.”\nMithl [like] here is like what occurs in your saying “[his like] is generous”: that is, loftiness itself is something of which envy is fair.\n[REVIEW T113-U3: The scan's grammatical example is truncated: مثل هاهنا مثل ما في قولك: يجود. The bracketed words supply its apparent point; check the quoted poetic/grammatical context.]\nThe sūrah is complete.","title":"“One who envies …”","author":"tibi","on":["K113.07"],"anchor":"K113.07","surah":113,"ar":"قولُه: (وما حاسدٌ) أولُه:\n\nوإني لمحسودٌ وأعذرُ حاسدي\n\nوقيل: أوله:\n\nهُمُ حَسَدوه -لا ملومين- مَجْدَه وما حاسدٌ في المكرمات بحاسد\n\nوقال:\n\nواعْذِرْ حَسودَك فيما قد خُصِصْتَ به إنّ العُلا حَسَنٌ في مثلِها الحسّدُ\n\nمِثْلُ ها هنا مثلُ ما في قولك: يجود. أي: إن العُلا حَسَنٌ فيها الحسَد.\n\nتَمَّتِ السُّورَة","html":"<p>His words, “One who envies …”: its opening is:\n“I am indeed envied, and I excuse my envier.”\nIt is also said that its opening is:\n“They envied him his glory—without being blameworthy;\none who envies noble accomplishments is no [blameworthy] envier.”\nHe also said:\n“Excuse your envier for what has been bestowed especially upon you:\nloftiness is such that envy of its like is fair.”\nMithl [like] here is like what occurs in your saying “[his like] is generous”: that is, loftiness itself is something of which envy is fair.\n[REVIEW T113-U3: The scan&#39;s grammatical example is truncated: <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">مثل هاهنا مثل ما في قولك</bdi>: <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">يجود</bdi>. The bracketed words supply its apparent point; check the quoted poetic/grammatical context.]\nThe sūrah is complete.</p>","lemma":"وما حاسد","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/final-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}}],"cairo_notes":[],"verses":[{"key":"113:1","number":1,"ar":"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَـٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ قُلْ أَعُوذُ بِرَبِّ ٱلْفَلَقِ","en":"Say: I seek refuge in the Lord of the Daybreak"},{"key":"113:2","number":2,"ar":"مِن شَرِّ مَا خَلَقَ","en":"From the evil of that which He created"},{"key":"113:3","number":3,"ar":"وَمِن شَرِّ غَاسِقٍ إِذَا وَقَبَ","en":"From the evil of the darkness when it is intense"},{"key":"113:4","number":4,"ar":"وَمِن شَرِّ ٱلنَّفَّـٰثَـٰتِ فِى ٱلْعُقَدِ","en":"And from the evil of malignant witchcraft"},{"key":"113:5","number":5,"ar":"وَمِن شَرِّ حَاسِدٍ إِذَا حَسَدَ","en":"And from the evil of the envier when he envieth"}],"quran_ar":"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَـٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ قُلْ أَعُوذُ بِرَبِّ ٱلْفَلَقِ ﴿1﴾ مِن شَرِّ مَا خَلَقَ ﴿2﴾ وَمِن شَرِّ غَاسِقٍ إِذَا وَقَبَ ﴿3﴾ وَمِن شَرِّ ٱلنَّفَّـٰثَـٰتِ فِى ٱلْعُقَدِ ﴿4﴾ وَمِن شَرِّ حَاسِدٍ إِذَا حَسَدَ ﴿5﴾","quran_en":"1. Say: I seek refuge in the Lord of the Daybreak 2. From the evil of that which He created 3. From the evil of the darkness when it is intense 4. And from the evil of malignant witchcraft 5. And from the evil of the envier when he envieth"}];
